window.SPECS_TR=window.SPECS_TR||{};window.SPECS_TR.en={"carpetas":{"framing":"Framing","insulacion":"Insulation","drywall":"Drywall installation","finishing":"Finishing","pintura":"Painting","fire2h":"2 h fire-rated walls"},"docs":{"01_092216_Framing_Metalico_No_Estructural.txt":{"titulo":"SECTION 09 22 16 – NON-STRUCTURAL METAL FRAMING","texto":"SECTION 09 22 16 – NON-STRUCTURAL METAL FRAMING\n========================\nSource: Manual V1, PDF pp. 745-754 (only version; not in Addendum 2, Addendum 3 or the RFIs reviewed).\nSource PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\nManufacturers and brands omitted on purpose (see PDF).\n\n1. SCOPE: framing for non-structural interior partitions, suspension of board ceilings and grid systems. Related: 054000 (cold-formed structural framing).\n2. DELEGATED DESIGN: by a professional engineer for non-standard partitions and for openings over 4 ft.\n3. DEFLECTION CRITERIA\n   - Interior walls: L/240 with a 5 psf lateral load.\n   - HVAC shafts and elevator shafts: 10 psf.\n   - Exterior walls: per structural drawings.\n   - With rigid finishes (e.g. tile): L/360.\n   - Ceilings: L/240 at 5 psf; with rigid finish L/360; steam room L/360 at 7.5 psf.\n4. MATERIALS\n   - Standards: ASTM C754 (installation) and C645 (components). G40 galvanizing; G90 in steam rooms.\n   - Studs and track: minimum thickness 0.0179\"; embossed/knurled studs minimum 0.0147\"; depth per drawings.\n   - Head joints that allow deflection, options:\n       · clip system that allows vertical movement (no dimension specified);\n       · single long-leg runner with 2\" flanges and bridging within 12\" of the top;\n       · double runner (inner runner with 2\" flanges);\n       · deflection track.\n   - Firestop track in rated walls.\n   - Track seals:\n       · Firestop seal (UL 2079): ±50 % movement; FS 10 / SD 20; Class 0 (G21); VOC 0.18.\n       · Smoke/acoustic seal: ±50 % movement; FS 5 / SD 40; VOC 0.20.\n   - Strap 0.0329\". Cold-rolled channel (CRC) bridging 0.0538\" with 1/2\" flanges; 1-1/2\" x 0.068\" clip angle.\n   - Hat channel 0.0179\"; 1/2\" resilient channel; CRC furring 0.053\"; brackets 0.0329\".\n   - Tie wire 0.062\" (or double 0.048\"). Z furring 1-1/4\" / 7/8\", 0.0179\".\n   - Ceiling suspension: wire hangers 0.16\"; flat hangers 1\" x 3/16\"; carrying channels 0.0538\";\n     3/4\" CRC furring at 0.0538\"; 7/8\" hat channel; anchors per ICC AC193/AC70; grid system.\n   - 1/8\" insulating foam gasket.\n5. EXECUTION\n   - Offset plates at ≤ 24\" o.c. before sprayed fireproofing (SFRM).\n   - Install per ASTM C754 and C840/C1063. Blocking and bracing; do not bridge building expansion joints.\n   - STUD SPACING: maximum 16\" o.c. for single-layer board, multilayer, tile backer, steam rooms and rigid finishes;\n     special finishes per the manufacturer.\n   - Isolation strip at exterior masonry walls. Flanges of all studs facing the same direction.\n   - Full-height framing to the structure, with slip joints.\n   - Doors: 2 studs per jamb; cripples with 1/2\" clearance; jamb studs anchored to the structure.\n   - Rated partitions continuous to the structure, with firestop track and seals; acoustic seals where indicated.\n   - Curved walls: studs at 6\" o.c. on the straight runs at the ends of the arc.\n   - Furring to concrete at 24\" o.c.; Z furring at 24\" o.c., with a second Z within 12\" of inside corners.\n   - BLOCKING (fire-retardant wood or sheet metal) for: framed openings, cabinets, plumbing fixtures,\n     toilet partitions, grab bars, toilet accessories, door hardware and audiovisual equipment.\n   - Elevator jamb struts. 0.0312\" strap for handrails/grab bars. Tolerance 1/8\".\n   - Ceiling suspension: hangers at 48\" o.c., carrying channels at 48\" o.c., furring at 16\" o.c.\n     Do not hang from roof deck, formwork, metal deck tabs or from ducts/piping. In rated assemblies, tie with wire.\n     Level within 1/8\" in 12 ft.\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed page number of the section).\n"},"02_054000_Framing_Acero_Formado_en_Frio.txt":{"titulo":"SECTION 05 40 00 – COLD-FORMED METAL FRAMING","texto":"SECTION 05 40 00 – COLD-FORMED METAL FRAMING\n========================\nCurrent version: Addendum 3, PDF pp. 169-181 (the text is the same as Manual V1, pp. 282-294).\nSource PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\nBrands omitted.\n\n1. SCOPE (Summary): load-bearing walls; non-load-bearing exterior walls; non-load-bearing interior walls outside the limits of 092216;\n   floor joists; rafters (roof); ceiling joists; soffits.\n2. SUBMITTALS: shop drawings, delegated design (engineer) and certificates.\n3. QUALITY: manufacturer that is an SFIA member and ICC AC98 certified; welding per AWS D1.1/D1.3.\n4. PERFORMANCE\n   - Deflection: exterior walls L/240; interior walls L/240 at 5 psf; ceiling joists L/240.\n   - Movement: temperature differential of 120 °F; vertical movement of ±3/4\". Exterior walls designed without sheathing contribution.\n   - Design standards AISI S100 and S240; UL ratings where applicable.\n5. MATERIALS\n   - Steel ASTM A1003 Type H, G60 coating (or CP60); G90 clips.\n   - Exterior studs and track: thickness 0.0428\", minimum flange 1-5/8\".\n   - Bypass clips; slotted deep-leg track; single/double deflection tracks 0.0428\".\n     The track flange appears as \"Insert dimension…\" (unedited text in the source).\n   - Interior studs: 0.0428\", flange 1-5/8\". Interior track: same thickness as the stud, flange 1-1/4\". Deflection tracks and drift clips.\n   - Ceiling joists and soffits: 0.0428\", flange 1-5/8\".\n   - Fasteners: ASTM F1554 Gr. 36 anchors; post-installed anchors ICC AC01/AC193; powder-actuated fastening AC70; ASTM C1513 screws.\n   - Galvanizing repair ASTM A780. Grout 1:2.5. Shims; 1/4\" neoprene gaskets.\n6. FABRICATION: screws with at least 3 threads exposed; torch cutting and wire tying are prohibited.\n   Tolerances: 1/8\" in 10 ft; spacing ±1/8\"; square within 1/8\"; panels within 1/16\".\n7. EXECUTION\n   - Shim or grout where the gap is > 1/4\"; install per ASTM C1007 / AISI S240.\n   - Insulate cavities that will become inaccessible; reinforcing plates at penetrations.\n   - STUD SPACING: \"As indicated on Drawings\" (per drawings; no value given).\n   - Bridging at ≤ 48\" vertically; top bridging within 12\" of a single deflection track.\n   - Joists: minimum bearing of 1-1/2\"; no more than 2\" from the walls.\n   - Weld testing by the owner.\nNote: Part 2 has no product articles for load-bearing walls, floor joists or rafters, although the Summary includes them (see FALTANTES.txt).\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed page number of the section).\n"},"03_092116.23_Shaft_Wall_Framing.txt":{"titulo":"SECTION 09 21 16.23 – GYPSUM BOARD SHAFT WALL: FRAMING","texto":"SECTION 09 21 16.23 – GYPSUM BOARD SHAFT WALL: FRAMING\n========================\nSource: Manual V1, PDF pp. 739-744 (only version). Source PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\nBrands omitted.\n\n- Submittals: ICC-ES evaluation reports for the systems.\n- Ratings: ASTM E119 (fire); acoustics E90/E413.\n- STUDS: standard profile of the system, minimum thickness 0.018\". J-RUNNER with long leg of at least 2\" and the same thickness as the studs.\n- FIRESTOP TRACK \"at head of shaft wall on each floor level\" (at the head of the shaft wall on each level).\n- ELEVATOR: J-jamb strut with 3\" leg and minimum thickness 0.033\".\n- Faces: 1\" shaftliner on the shaft side (moisture-resistant Type X or extended exposure); room-side board per 092900.\n- Framing ASTM C645, G40 galvanizing. Screws ASTM C1002.\n- Anchors with capacity of 5 times the load (ASTM E488); powder-actuated fastening with 10 times the load (ASTM E1190).\n- Acoustic sealant 079219 and SAB (sound attenuation blanket).\nEXECUTION\n- Coordinate with sprayed fireproofing (SFRM).\n- Install per ASTM C754 \"other than stud-spacing requirements\" (except the stud spacing requirements, which the system gives).\n  Sheet A8.1, note 1, indicates shaft wall studs at 24\" o.c. unless noted.\n- Do not bridge expansion joints. Supplementary framing where required. Elevator jamb struts.\n- 0.033\" strap for handrails. Penetrations must maintain the rating.\n- Isolate the perimeter; firestop tracks; control joints per C840; acoustic sealing.\n- Cant panels at projections over 4\": 1/2\" or 5/8\" board, angle ≥ 75°, screws at 24\" o.c., framing at 24\" o.c.\n- Tolerance 1/8\".\nSheet A8.1: types H2-4-30 (6 1/4\", 2 h) and H2-6-30 (7 1/4\", 2 h); Detail 2B \"Detail at Shaft Walls\"; Details 3 and 5 \"Elevator Shaft Detail\"\n  (C-H studs with 1\" shaftliner). Sheet A7.12: detail \"Elevator Door Head and Jamb Detail at Shaft Wall\".\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed page number of the section).\n"},"04_061053_Carpinteria_Rough_y_Blocking.txt":{"titulo":"SECTION 06 10 53 – MISCELLANEOUS ROUGH CARPENTRY – BLOCKING","texto":"SECTION 06 10 53 – MISCELLANEOUS ROUGH CARPENTRY – BLOCKING\n========================\nCurrent version: Addendum 2, PDF pp. 36-43. Changes from Manual V1:\n  - New 2.04 G: coordinate preservative-treated wood with the membranes.\n  - New 2.08 A.3: Type 304 stainless steel fasteners where exposed to weather, in ground contact,\n    in preservative-treated wood or with high relative humidity.\nSource PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\nSection 06 10 00 \"Rough Carpentry\" does not exist; this is the only rough carpentry section (see FALTANTES.txt).\n\n1. SCOPE: curbs; blocking, cants and nailers; furring and grounds; sleepers; plywood backing.\n2. LUMBER: DOC PS 20; moisture ≤ 19 %.\n3. PRESERVATIVE TREATMENT: AWPA U1, categories UC2 / UC3b / UC4a; no arsenic or chromium; do not use SBX on sill plates. Kiln-dry to 19 %.\n   The section lists which items are treated. Use series 300 stainless steel fasteners and flashing in contact with treated wood.\n4. FIRE-RETARDANT-TREATED WOOD (FRT)\n   - Flame spread index ≤ 25 in the extended 30 min test; flame front ≤ 10.5 ft.\n   - Exterior type tested per ASTM D2898. Interior type A: ≤ 28 % moisture at 92 % RH.\n   - Design factors 0.85 / 0.75. Kiln-drying: lumber 19 %, plywood 15 %.\n   - FRT required for: carpentry within rated assemblies, raised platforms and plywood backing.\n     Not on roofing, unless the roof manufacturer confirms it.\n5. MISCELLANEOUS LUMBER: Construction or No. 2 grade (species list); concealed boards at 19 % moisture.\n6. PLYWOOD BACKING: FRT, minimum thickness 3/4\".\n7. FASTENERS\n   - Connectors G185 / ASTM A153 Class D; stainless connectors 316 / A153 Class C; stainless 304 (see Addendum 2 change).\n   - Nails ASTM F1667. Screws ASTM C1002 for non-structural steel and C954 for cold-formed steel.\n   - Powder-actuated fastening ICC AC70; anchors AC01 / AC58 / AC193 / AC308.\n   - Adhesive ASTM D3498 without urea-formaldehyde. Flexible flashing ≥ 0.025\".\n8. EXECUTION\n   - Per WCD 1. FRT plywood installed with the mark visible.\n   - Metal clips at ≤ 16\" o.c. at gypsum board corners.\n   - Fire blocking: at each floor and ceiling and at ≤ 96\" o.c.; sleepers in areas ≤ 100 ft²; cornices at ≤ 20 ft.\n   - Field treatment of cuts per AWPA M4. Separator flashing over metal deck.\n   - Fastening per IBC Table 2304.9.1. Treated wood must not touch the metal deck (use membrane or untreated heartwood fir).\n   - PLYWOOD BACKING: screws at ≤ 24\" o.c.; in rated walls, over the rated board; 48\" x 96\" sheets horizontal\n     at ceiling height; no gaps.\n   - Protect SBX-treated wood with borate.\nRelation to 092216: blocking in metal stud walls may be FRT wood or sheet metal (092216 Execution).\nSheet A8.1, note 13: walls supporting cabinets, shelves, grab bars, equipment and wall accessories shall be reinforced per the\naccessory manufacturer and the latest edition of the state building code, whichever is greater.\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed page number of the section).\n"},"05_A8.1_Tipos_de_Pared_Notas_y_Cedulas.txt":{"titulo":"SHEET A8.1 – PARTITION TYPES AND SCHEDULES, REVISION RFI-A8.1","texto":"SHEET A8.1 – PARTITION TYPES AND SCHEDULES, REVISION RFI-A8.1\n========================\nSource: \"A8.1 - PARTITION TYPES AND SCHEDULES - RFI-A8.1\" (1 p.). Source PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\nThe sheet lists several revisions (addenda and RFIs; numbers omitted).\n\nGENERAL NOTES (faithful translation)\n 1. All standard stud framing at 16\" o.c. unless noted; all SHAFT WALL framing at 24\" o.c. unless noted.\n 2. Fire rating per the code drawings.\n 3. All board 5/8\" Type X unless noted; paint all exposed faces.\n 4. Water-resistant fiberglass Type X backer board instead of the final board layer, where indicated in the schedule types,\n    at interior wet walls (showers, restrooms) and at non-rated, non-acoustic tub walls.\n 5. Moisture- and mold-resistant Type X board at all interior \"wet\" locations, including bathrooms.\n 6. The wall type above or below an opening is the same as that on either side of it.\n 7. Different wall types align so that wall planes continue without a break within rooms, unless noted.\n 8. Interior wall dimensions are to the face of gypsum board.\n    After note 8, the sheet has this paragraph without its own number: rated walls form a continuous separation from floor to floor,\n    without interruption at columns, beams, transitions or obstructions; in rated conditions, penetrations are sealed with firestop sealant.\n 9. Insulation the full width of the stud, up to 6\" deep maximum in walls; R-19 unfaced at interior furring next to uninsulated\n    exterior walls (concrete, CMU, spandrel panels at windows/curtain walls); continuous around furred columns and obstructions.\n10. 25 GAUGE STUDS at all single-stud acoustic walls, unless noted. Use knurled studs or adjust spacing for the span.\n    Spacing may NOT be less than 16\" o.c. without architect approval (text: \"SPACING SHALL BE NO LESS THAN 16\" O.C.\").\n    Heavier gauge may be used at non-acoustic or double-stud walls.\n    MAXIMUM SPANS based on L/240 deflection and a 5 psf design load, unless noted.\n11. Acoustic walls get a blanket the full width of the stud and are sealed airtight with acoustic sealant between board and slabs, beams,\n    columns, walls or steel, at penetrations and at dissimilar materials. They are acoustic, unless noted:\n    a) unit and retail demising walls; b) corridor walls; c) exterior walls (thermal insulation);\n    d) stud bays with plumbing; e) plumbing chase walls; f) bathroom walls; g) shaft walls in units;\n    h) spandrel and furred exterior walls (thermal); i) walls between conditioned and unconditioned spaces (thermal);\n    j) walls in public/common areas, including residential lobbies and enclosed rooms.\n12. Fire-resistant joint system between walls and fire-resistant floors/ceilings, per [state code] 2015, Section 706.\n13. Walls with cabinets, shelves, grab bars, equipment and accessories: reinforce per the accessory manufacturer and the current building code, whichever is greater.\n\nTYPE KEY (sample tag \"SA.3.10\": material . rating/height . width . board indicator . unique condition)\n  Material: S = steel (metal studs); W = wood studs; H = shaft wall; M = masonry; C = concrete.\n  Rating/height: A = full height to structure; B = 6\" above ceiling; C = to underside of ceiling; D = partial height;\n    S = smoke partition; Y = varies in height (all non-rated); 0 = 0 h (corridor); 5 = 0.5 h; 1, 2, 3, 4 = hours of rating.\n  Width: F = furring/channels; 1 = 1 5/8\" metal; 2 = 2 1/2\" metal / 1 1/2\" wood; 3 = 3 5/8\" metal; 4 = 4\" metal / 3 1/2\" wood / 3 5/8\" CMU / 4\" concrete;\n    6 = 6\" metal / 5 1/2\" wood / 5 5/8\" CMU / 6\" concrete; 8 = 8\" metal / 7 1/4\" wood / 7 5/8\" CMU / 8\" concrete; 10 = 10\" concrete;\n    12 = 11 5/8\" CMU / 12\" concrete; D = double stud; S = staggered studs; Y = non-standard width.\n  Board (no shaftliner): 0 = no board; 1 = one layer on tag side; 2 = one layer each side; 3 = two layers tag side and one on the other;\n    4 = two layers each side; 5 = two layers tag side and none on the other; 6 = three layers each side.\n  Unique condition: 0 = none; 1 = load-bearing stud (mark omitted); 2 = sound attenuation blanket (SAB); 3 = resilient channel + SAB; 4 = tile backer board;\n    5 = concrete wall with corrugated sheet formwork; 6 = typical framing at underside of joist; 7 = concrete wall with corrugated formwork\n    + resilient channel with board on the stair side; 8 = tile backer board with air and vapor barrier.\n  Load-bearing studs are marked with hatching on the plans (series A1/A2, not available).\n  Types graphic: \"NO LIMITING HEIGHT IF BRACED TO ADJACENT CONSTRUCTION\" (no limiting height if braced to adjacent construction).\n\nSCHEDULES (overall thickness; rating; STC/comment). The tested assembly, top/bottom detail columns are blank except \"UL ###\".\n  Non-rated SA-X-1X: SA-1-10 and SA-1-16 varies; SA-3-10 varies; SA-3-12 and SA-3-18 4 1/4\"; SA-6-10/12/18 6 5/8\".\n  Non-rated SA-X-2X: SA-3-20/22/28 4 7/8\"; SA-6-20/22/28 7 1/4\"; SD-3-20 4 7/8\".\n  1 h S1-X-2X: S1-3-10 4 1/4\"; S1-3-20 4 7/8\"; S1-3-23 varies, STC 64 (residential/fitness separation); S1-6-13 7 1/8\", STC 56 (typical corridor/demising);\n    S1-6-20 and S1-6-22 7 1/4\"; S1-6-23 7 3/4\", STC 56 (typical corridor/demising); S1-6-42 8 1/2\"; S1-8-20 9 1/4\".\n  2 h S2-X-4X: S2-3-40 6 1/8\"; S2-6-40 and S2-6-41 8 1/2\".\n  Shaft 2 h H2-X-3X: H2-4-30 6 1/4\"; H2-6-30 7 1/4\".\n  Masonry: M1-8-00 7 5/8\" (1 h); M2-8-00 7 5/8\" (2 h); M4-8-00 7 5/8\" (4 h); M0-8-00 7 5/8\" (0 h).\n  Non-rated concrete: CA-6-00 6\"; CA-12-00 varies. 2 h concrete: C2-6-00 6\"; C2-8-00 8\" (UL ###); C2-10-00 10\"; C2-10-07 1'-0 5/8\" (beton wall);\n    C2-11-05 1'-0 1/8\" (UL ###); C2-11-07 1'-1 5/8\" (UL ###); C2-12-00 1'-0\"; C2-18-00 1'-6\" (UL ###).\nDETAILS: 2B \"Detail at Shaft Walls\"; 3 and 5 \"Elevator Shaft Detail\". Legend: sprayed fireproofing (UL number to be verified by the metal framing subcontractor).\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed page number of the section).\n"},"06_A3.21_Framing_de_Plafones.txt":{"titulo":"SHEET A3.21 – CEILING DETAILS, IFC: FRAMING","texto":"SHEET A3.21 – CEILING DETAILS, IFC: FRAMING\n========================\nSource: \"A3.21-CEILING DETAILS-IFC\" (1 p.). Source PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\n\n- Note 10: walls go to the underside of the slab, except shaded walls, where the board extends 4\" above the ceiling.\n  ALL metal studs extend to the underside of the floor or roof slab.\n- Ceiling types: GWB-1 = board on metal studs hung from the rated floor/ceiling assembly; GWB-2 = attached to the structure (see A6.0);\n  GWB-3 = 2 h rated ceiling on metal studs.\n- Typical board ceiling: 5/8\" board; 3 5/8\" studs at 16\" o.c.; bracing at 32\" o.c.\n- Studs at 16\" o.c., with depth for L/240 maximum, unless noted.\n- 1C: 1\" expansion joint with continuous cover.\n- 1D: perimeter casing bead held 1/4\" off the wall, with backer rod and sealant.\n- 2A Control joint: 1 1/2\" channels; continuous furring at 4\" maximum each side; 1/4\" gap; 16\" overlap of nested channels\n  with 2 wire ties per side; inverted furring splice.\n- 2C/2D Access panel: compound at the perimeter of the opening; opening = panel width + 1/4\"; SAB held with strap.\n- 2E Box header: 1/4\" stainless steel screws at 12\" o.c. (\"verify\" on the sheet).\nSee also 092216 (suspension: hangers and carrying channels at 48\" o.c., furring at 16\" o.c.).\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed page number of the section).\n"},"07_A7_Escaleras_Elevador_y_Hoistway.txt":{"titulo":"SHEETS A7.x – STAIRS, ELEVATOR AND HOISTWAY: FRAMING","texto":"SHEETS A7.x – STAIRS, ELEVATOR AND HOISTWAY: FRAMING\n========================\nSources: \"A7.10-STAIR AND ELEVATOR DETAILS-IFC\" and \"A7.12-ELEVATOR DETAILS-IFC\" (1 p. each).\nSource PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\n- A7.10: \"GWB ON METAL STUDS BETWEEN STRINGERS AND ALONG BEAM/BELOW LANDING - SEE SECTIONS\"\n  (board on metal studs between stringers and along the beam and below the landing; see sections).\n  \"CASING BEAD AND 1/4\" SEALANT WHERE GWB OCCURS\" (casing bead and 1/4\" sealant where there is board).\n- A7.12: detail \"ELEVATOR DOOR HEAD AND JAMB DETAIL AT SHAFT WALL\" (elevator door head and jamb at shaft wall).\n  The J-jamb strut requirements (3\" leg, ≥ 0.033\") are in 092116.23 (file 03).\n- Sheets A7.2, A7.3 (RFI-A8.1) and A7.4 (plans and sections of stair 2 and hoistway) were reviewed and have no framing text.\n  They were not copied.\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed page number of the section).\n"},"08_092900_Requisitos_de_la_Placa_sobre_el_Framing.txt":{"titulo":"SECTION 09 29 00 (RFI-092900) – REQUIREMENTS AFFECTING THE FRAMING","texto":"SECTION 09 29 00 (RFI-092900) – REQUIREMENTS AFFECTING THE FRAMING\n========================\nSource: \"092900 - GYPSUM BOARD - RFI-092900\". Source PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\n(pp. 7-9; the screws are in 2.3, p. 6, which is included in the full copy in the finishing folder).\n- 3.1 Examination (p.7): before placing the board, verify that framing, blocking, insulation and firestopping are complete and correct.\n- Screws per framing thickness (2.3): ASTM C1002 for framing under 0.033\"; ASTM C954 for 0.033\" to 0.112\"; type W wood with 3/4\" penetration.\n- 3.3.A (p.8): fasten the board to framing and furring per the manufacturer, GA-216 or ASTM C840.\n- 3.3.E (p.8): where the board runs full height to the structure, provide clearance for deflection by trimming the board 3/8\" (nominal)\n  and sealing the top edge to the substrate with a continuous bead of sealant, which forms an elastic seal.\n- 3.3.F: fasten the board to the framing with screws.\n- Control joints (3.3, p.8-9): at changes of framing direction and at the transition from floor-supported framing to suspended framing.\n- 3.5 (p.9): sound-deadening board screwed at 16\" o.c. minimum.\n- 3.7 (p.10, in the finishing folder): cement board with additional blocking where required; edges parallel to framing with continuous support.\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed page number of the section).\n"},"09_Resumen_Espaciado_Calibres_y_Deflexion.txt":{"titulo":"COMPILED SUMMARY – STUD SPACING, THICKNESSES/GAUGES, TRACK AND DEFLECTION","texto":"COMPILED SUMMARY – STUD SPACING, THICKNESSES/GAUGES, TRACK AND DEFLECTION\n========================\nOnly what the sources say is compiled. Each item carries its source (see files 01-08).\n\nSTUD SPACING\n  - Standard studs: 16\" o.c. unless noted ...................................... A8.1 note 1\n  - Shaft wall: 24\" o.c. unless noted ........................................... A8.1 note 1 (092116.23 refers to the system)\n  - Maximum 16\" o.c. with one layer, multilayer, tile backer, steam room and rigid finishes ... 092216 Execution\n  - Single-stud acoustic walls: \"no less than 16\" o.c.\" without approval ....... A8.1 note 10\n  - Board ceilings: 3 5/8\" studs at 16\" o.c., bracing at 32\" o.c. .............. A3.21\n  - Structural framing 054000: \"As indicated on Drawings\" (no value) ........... 054000 Execution\n  - Curved walls: 6\" o.c. at the ends of the arc ................................ 092216\n  - Furring to concrete and Z-furring: 24\" o.c. .................................. 092216\n  - Suspension: hangers 48\" o.c., carrying channels 48\", furring 16\" ........... 092216\nTHICKNESSES / GAUGES\n  - Non-structural studs: ≥ 0.0179\"; embossed ≥ 0.0147\" ........................ 092216\n  - 25 gauge studs at single-stud acoustic walls ................................. A8.1 note 10\n  - Shaft wall: studs ≥ 0.018\"; long-leg J-runner ≥ 2\"; 3\" elevator J-jamb, ≥ 0.033\" ... 092116.23\n  - Cold-formed (054000): exterior and interior studs/track 0.0428\" (1-5/8\" flange; interior track 1-1/4\" flange);\n    deflection tracks 0.0428\"; ceiling joists and soffits 0.0428\" ............... 054000\n  - Nominal widths: 1 5/8\", 2 1/2\", 3 5/8\", 4\", 6\", 8\" (type key) .............. A8.1\nTRACK / HEAD OF WALL\n  - Long-leg runner with 2\" flanges + bridging within 12\" of the top; double runner; clips; deflection track ... 092216\n  - Firestop track and seals (UL 2079, ±50 %) at rated walls ................... 092216\n  - Firestop track at the shaft wall head at each level ........................ 092116.23\n  - Top bridging within 12\" of a single deflection track; bridging ≤ 48\" vertical ... 054000\n  - Design vertical movement ±3/4\" ................................................ 054000\n  - Board trimmed 3/8\" and sealed where it reaches the structure (deflection) ..... 092900 RFI-092900\n  - All studs to the underside of the slab ...................................... A3.21 note 10\nDEFLECTION\n  - Interior walls L/240 at 5 psf ................................ 092216, 054000 and A8.1 note 10\n  - Rigid finishes L/360 ........................................... 092216\n  - HVAC and elevator shafts: 10 psf ............................... 092216\n  - Exterior walls L/240 (054000); exterior per structural drawings (092216)\n  - Ceilings L/240 at 5 psf; rigid L/360; steam room L/360 at 7.5 psf ... 092216. Ceiling joists L/240 ... 054000. Ceilings L/240 ... A3.21\nTOLERANCES\n  - 1/8\" (092216 and 092116.23); 1/8\" in 10 ft (054000); ceiling level 1/8\" in 12 ft (092216).\n"},"04_bastidor_metalico_092216.txt":{"titulo":"NON-STRUCTURAL METAL FRAMING - SECTION 092216 (V1 p745-754)","texto":"NON-STRUCTURAL METAL FRAMING - SECTION 092216 (V1 p745-754)\n(assembly details for studs, tracks, furring and suspension)\n\n1. SCOPE (1.02.A, p745)\n- \"Non-load-bearing steel framing systems for interior partitions.\"\n- \"Suspension systems for interior ceilings and soffits.\"\n- \"Grid suspension systems for gypsum board ceilings.\"\n\n2. DEFLECTION / PERFORMANCE (2.02, p746-747)\n- \"Horizontal Deflection - Typical: For wall assemblies, limited to 1/240 of the wall height based on horizontal loading as follows: Typical: 5 lbf/sq. ft. / Shaft Walls at HVAC Shafts: 10 lbf/sq. ft. / Shaft Walls at Elevator Shafts: 10 lbf/sq. ft.\"\n- \"Horizontal Deflection - Rigid Applied Surface Materials: ... limited to 1/360 of the wall height\" (same loads).\n- Ceilings: \"limited to 1/240 of the span based on loading of 5 lbf/sq. ft.\"; rigid 1/360; steam rooms 1/360 at 7.5 lbf/sq. ft.\n\n3. MATERIALS (2.03, p747-749)\n- Studs and runners: \"ASTM C 645. Use either steel studs and runners or embossed steel studs and runners.\"\n- Steel studs: \"Minimum Base-Metal Thickness: As required by performance requirements for horizontal deflection but at minimum 0.0179 inch.\" ; \"Depth: As indicated on Drawings.\"\n- Embossed studs: \"... but at minimum 0.0147 inch.\"\n- Coating: \"G40, hot-dip galvanized\"; steam rooms \"G90\".\n- Flat strap / backing plate: \"Minimum Base-Metal Thickness: 0.0329 inch.\"\n- Cold-rolled channel bridging: \"0.0538-inch minimum base-metal thickness, with minimum 1/2-inch- wide flanges.\" ; clip angle \"1-1/2 by 1-1/2 inches, 0.068-inch- thick\".\n- Hat-shaped rigid furring: \"ASTM C 645. Minimum Base-Metal Thickness: 0.0179 inch.\"\n- Z-furring: \"face flange of 1-1/4 inches, wall attachment flange of 7/8 inch, minimum uncoated-metal thickness of 0.0179 inch\".\n- Isolation strip (2.05.B, p750): \"Adhesive-backed, closed-cell vinyl foam strips ... 1/8 inch thick\".\n- A8.1 N10: \"STUDS TO BE 25 GA AT ALL SINGLE-STUD ACOUSTICAL WALLS U.O.N.\"\n\n4. GENERAL INSTALLATION (3.03, p751)\n- \"Installation Standard: ASTM C 754.\" / \"Gypsum Board Assemblies: Also comply with requirements in ASTM C 840 that apply to framing installation.\"\n- \"Install framing and accessories plumb, square, and true to line, with connections securely fastened.\"\n- \"Install bracing at terminations in assemblies.\"\n- \"Do not bridge building control and expansion joints with non-load-bearing steel framing members. Frame both sides of joints independently.\"\n\n5. STUD SPACING (3.04.A, p751-752)\n- \"Single-Layer Application: As required by deflection performance requirements; but at maximum 16 inches o.c.\"\n- \"Multilayer Application: ... but at maximum 16 inches o.c.\"\n- \"Tile Backing Panels: ... but at maximum 16 inches o.c.\"\n- \"Assemblies to Receive Rigid Applied Surface Materials: ... but at maximum 16 inches o.c.\"\n- A8.1 N1: standard 16\" o.c.; shaft wall 24\" o.c. UNO.\n\n6. ASSEMBLY (3.04.B-D, p752)\n- \"Where studs are installed directly against exterior masonry walls or dissimilar metals at exterior walls, install isolation strip between studs and exterior wall.\"\n- \"Install studs so flanges within framing system point in same direction.\"\n- \"Install tracks (runners) at floors and overhead supports. Extend framing full height to structural supports or substrates above suspended ceilings except where partitions are indicated to terminate at suspended ceilings.\"\n- Doors: \"Screw vertical studs at jambs to jamb anchor clips on door frames\"; \"Install two studs at each jamb unless otherwise indicated.\"; cripple studs \"with a minimum 1/2-inch clearance from jamb stud to allow for installation of control joint\"; \"Extend jamb studs through suspended ceilings and attach to underside of overhead structure.\"\n- Curved partitions: \"On straight lengths of no fewer than two studs at ends of arcs, place studs 6 inches o.c.\"\n- Direct furring (p752-753): concrete/masonry \"spaced 24 inches o.c.\"\n- Z-furring (p753): \"spaced 24 inches o.c.\"; inside corner \"no more than 12 inches from corner\".\n- Tolerance (3.04.H, p753): \"Install each framing member so fastening surfaces vary not more than 1/8 inch from the plane formed by faces of adjacent framing.\"\n\n7. CEILING SUSPENSION (3.05, p753-754)\n- \"Hangers: 48 inches o.c.\" / \"Carrying Channels (Main Runners): 48 inches o.c.\" / \"Furring Channels (Furring Members): 16 inches o.c.\"\n- \"Fire-Resistance-Rated Assemblies: Wire tie furring channels to supports.\"\n- \"Do not attach hangers to steel roof deck.\" ; \"Do not connect or suspend steel framing from ducts, pipes, or conduit.\"\n- Tolerance: \"level to within 1/8 inch in 12 feet\".\n\n8. COLD-FORMED (054000, A3 p171-180) - related\n- A3 p171: \"Interior Non-Load-Bearing Framing: Horizontal deflection of 1/240 of the wall height under a horizontal load of 5 lbf/sq. ft.\"\n- A3 p179 3.5.B: \"Fasten both flanges of studs to top and bottom track unless otherwise indicated.\" ; \"Stud Spacing: As indicated on Drawings.\"\n- A3 p179-180: bridging \"not more than 48 inches apart. Fasten at each stud intersection.\"\n\n9. NOT FOUND\n- Specific stud gauge/thickness for 2 h walls (S2, H2): NOT FOUND (only minimums and \"as indicated\").\n- Limiting height per wall type: NOT FOUND (only the note \"NO LIMITING HEIGHT IF BRACED TO ADJACENT CONSTRUCTION\" in shaft detail A8.1).\n"},"05_RC1_resilient_channel.txt":{"titulo":"RC1 / RESILIENT CHANNEL","texto":"RC1 / RESILIENT CHANNEL\n\n1. TERM \"RC1\" / \"RC-1\"\n- NOT FOUND as resilient channel in any source.\n- Only match \"RC-1\": V2 p113 \"Type RC-1 (Roof Curb, Type 1)\" -> not drywall.\n\n2. SPECIFICATION (092216)\n- 2.03.J (V1 p749): \"Resilient Furring Channels: 1/2-inch- deep, steel sheet members designed to reduce sound transmission.\"\n  \"Configuration: Asymmetrical or hat shaped.\"\n- 2.04.F.5 (V1 p750, suspension): \"Resilient Furring Channels: 1/2-inch- deep members designed to reduce sound transmission.\"\n  \"Configuration: Asymmetrical or hat shaped.\"\n- Furring spacing in ceilings (3.05.A.3, V1 p753): \"Furring Channels (Furring Members): 16 inches o.c.\" (generic, not specific to resilient).\n\n3. WHERE IT APPLIES (A8.1)\n- Unique condition 3: \"RESILIENT CHANNELS W/ SOUND ATTENUATION BLANKET\"\n- Unique condition 7: \"... CORRUGATED METAL FORMWORK AND RESILIENT CHANNEL W/ GWB FINISH ON STAIR SIDE\"\n- Detail legend: \"RESILIENT CHANNEL (WHERE UNIQUE CONDITION MODIFIER 3 IS INDICATED)\"\n- Detail legend: \"... W/ FURRING CHANNEL AND GYPSUM BOARD FINISH ON STAIR INTERIOR SIDE (WHERE UNIQUE CONDITION MODIFIER 7 IS INDICATED)\"\n- Scheduled types with modifier 3: S1-3-23 (11 3/4\", STC 64, \"RESIDENTIAL / FITNESS SEPARATION\") and S1-6-23 (7 3/4\", STC 56, \"TYPICAL CORRIDOR / DEMISING\").\n- Types with modifier 7: C2-10-07 and C2-11-07 (2 h concrete).\n\n4. NOT FOUND\n- Gauge / metal thickness of the resilient channel: NOT FOUND (only 1/2\" depth).\n- Resilient channel spacing on walls: NOT FOUND.\n- Screws to fasten RC to the stud and board to the RC (type, length, spacing): NOT FOUND.\n- Installation notes (avoid short-circuit, orientation, distance to floor/ceiling): NOT FOUND.\n- RC manufacturer/product: NOT FOUND.\n"},"11_deflection_head_of_wall.txt":{"titulo":"DEFLECTION TRACKS / HEAD-OF-WALL","texto":"DEFLECTION TRACKS / HEAD-OF-WALL\n\n1. 092216 HEAD JOINTS (2.03.D, V1 p748)\n- \"Slip-Type Head Joints: Where indicated, provide one of the following:\"\n- \"Clip System: Clips designed for use in head-of-wall deflection conditions that provide a positive attachment of studs to tracks while allowing 1-1/2-inch minimum vertical movement.\"\n- \"Single Long-Leg Runner System: ASTM C 645 top runner with 2-inch- deep flanges in thickness not less than indicated for studs, installed with studs friction fit into top runner and with continuous bridging located within 12 inches of the top of studs to provide lateral bracing.\"\n- \"Double-Runner System: ASTM C 645 top runners, inside runner with 2-inch- deep flanges in thickness not less than indicated for studs and fastened to studs, and outer runner sized to friction fit inside runner.\"\n- \"Deflection Track: Steel sheet top runner manufactured to prevent cracking of finishes applied to interior partition framing resulting from deflection of structure above; in thickness not less than indicated for studs and in width to accommodate depth of studs.\"\n- 2.03.E: \"Firestop Tracks: Top runner ... designed to allow partition heads to expand and contract with movement of structure while maintaining continuity of fire-resistance-rated assembly indicated; in thickness not less than indicated for studs and in width to accommodate depth of studs.\"\n- 2.03.G: smoke and acoustic track seal (product omitted).\n- 3.04.D.1 (V1 p752): \"Slip-Type Head Joints: Where framing extends to overhead structural supports, install to produce joints at tops of framing systems that prevent axial loading of finished assemblies.\"\n\n2. 092900 - TOP CLEARANCE (3.3.E, V1 p762 / RFI-092900 p8)\n- \"Where gypsum board abuts dissimilar materials and at the top of walls, provide a joint by undercutting board 3/8 inch (nominal) and sealing top edge to adjacent surface with a continuous bead of sealant to form an elastic closure.\"\n\n3. 092116.23 (V1 p741)\n- \"Firestop Tracks: Provide firestop track at head of shaft wall on each floor level.\"\n\n4. 054000 (Addendum 3)\n- A3 p171: \"Design framing system to maintain clearances at openings, to allow for construction tolerances, and to accommodate live load deflection of primary building structure as follows: Upward and downward movement of 3/4 inch.\"\n- A3 p172-174: \"Slotted Deflection Track\", \"Single Deflection Track: Manufacturer's single, deep-leg, U-shaped steel track; unpunched\", \"Double Deflection Tracks: Manufacturer's double, deep-leg, U-shaped steel tracks\"; \"Minimum Base-Steel Thickness: 0.0428 inch.\"\n- A3 p179: \"Top Bridging for Single Deflection Track: Install row of horizontal bridging within 12 inches of single deflection track.\"\n\n5. 078400\n- 2.03.B (V1 p538): head-of-wall between non-rated floor and rated wall: \"tested according to ASTM E2837\".\n- Table 3.08: \"HW\" = Head of Wall (guide only).\n\n6. A3.21\n- N10: \"ALL METAL STUDS EXTEND TO UNDERSIDE OF FLOOR OR ROOF DECK.\"\n- 1E: \"METAL STUDS @ 16\" O.C., DEPTH TO RESULT IN DEFLECTION OF L/240 MAX, UNO\"\n- 1D: \"PERIMETER CASING BEAD - HOLD OFF THE WALL TO PROVIDE 1/4\" GAP\" ; \"BACKER ROD AND SEALANT\"\n\n7. NOT FOUND\n- Graphic head-of-wall detail (A8.1 \"T.O. WALL DETAIL\" columns blank): NOT FOUND.\n- Specific deflection gap (e.g. 3/4\" or 1\") for partitions under slab: NOT FOUND in 092216 (only clip 1-1/2\" min. movement and 3/4\" in 054000).\n"},"13_blocking_refuerzos.txt":{"titulo":"BLOCKING AND REINFORCEMENT","texto":"BLOCKING AND REINFORCEMENT\n\n1. 092216 3.04.G (V1 p753)\n- \"Install fire treated wood blocking or mechanically fastened sheet metal blocking\" for: framed openings, casework/cabinets, plumbing fixtures, toilet partitions, grab bars, toilet accessories, door hardware and AV equipment (abridged list).\n- Handrails/grab bars: \"reinforcing strip\" \"0.0312 inch\" (summary; see section).\n- 2.03.H (V1 p749): \"Flat Strap and Backing Plate: Steel sheet for blocking and bracing ... Minimum Base-Metal Thickness: 0.0329 inch.\"\n\n2. 092116.23 3.03.D (V1 p743)\n- \"provide galvanized steel reinforcing strip with 0.033-inch minimum thickness of base metal (uncoated), accurately positioned and secured behind at least one layer of face panel.\"\n\n3. 092900 3.1 (V1 p761 / RFI-092900 p7-8)\n- Verify blocking is installed before placing board (summary).\n\n4. A8.1 N13\n- \"WALL SUPPORTING CASEWORK, SHELVING, GRAB BARS, EQUIPMENT AND OTHER WALL-MOUNTED FIXTURES SHALL BE RINFORCED PER THE FIXTURE MANUFACTURER'S REQUIREMENTS AND THE REQUIREMENTS OF THE LATEST EDITION OF THE [STATE BUILDING CODE], WHICHEVER IS GREATER.\"\n\n5. NOT FOUND\n- Blocking detail (heights, dimensions, fastening): NOT FOUND.\n- Interior elevation drawings with blocking locations: NOT FOUND.\n"},"12_aislamiento_acustico.txt":{"titulo":"INSULATION (SOUND ATTENUATION) AND ACOUSTIC SEALING","texto":"INSULATION (SOUND ATTENUATION) AND ACOUSTIC SEALING\n\n1. A8.1\n- N9: \"INSULATION SHALL BE FULL STUD WIDTH (MAX 6\"). PROVIDE UNFACED R-19 BATT INSULATION AT FURRING ADJACENT TO UNINSULATED EXTERIOR WALLS. INSULATION SHALL BE CONTINUOUS AROUND FURRED COLUMNS.\"\n- N11: acoustic walls with full-width batt, sealed airtight with acoustic sealant; applies to: a) demising, b) corridor, c) exterior, d) plumbing bays, e) pony walls, f) bathroom, g) \"SHAFT WALLS AT UNITS\", h) spandrel, i) conditioned/unconditioned, j) public areas (abridged list; see sheet).\n- Unique condition 2: sound attenuation blanket; 3: \"RESILIENT CHANNELS W/ SOUND ATTENUATION BLANKET\".\n- Scheduled STC: S1-3-23 = 64; S1-6-13 = 56; S1-6-23 = 56.\n\n2. 098100 ACOUSTIC INSULATION (A2 p156-160; V1 p828-832)\n- Batt: \"ASTM C665 Type I unfaced\", density \"0.60 pcf\"; \"Select batt thickness to fill cavity airspace\".\n- 3.3.A.1: \"Sound batt insulation is required to be installed in floors, unit demising walls, corridor wall assemblies, bathroom walls, and all bedroom walls.\"\n- 3.3.A.2: STC 64 required in amenity, fitness, loading dock and mechanical areas (summary).\n- Penetrations: \"Any gap larger than 1/2-inch shall be covered with gypsum board, lapped a minimum of 2-inches, and screwed before using acoustical sealant.\"\n\n3. 072100 THERMAL INSULATION (A3 p227-235)\n- Metal cavities >96\": \"support unfaced blankets mechanically\" (summary of A3 p234-235).\n- \"Insulation in Metal Framed Walls: Mineral-wool blanket or glass-fiber blanket insulation.\"\n\n4. 092900 SOUND DAMPING (3.5, V1 p763 / RFI-092900 p9)\n- Perimeter clearance of 1/4\" to 1/2\" with acoustic sealant; \"Comply with ASTM C919\"; screws \"16 inches o.c.\" minimum; sealant bead 1/8\" to 3/8\"; putty behind boxes (summary; see 01_patron_tornillos.txt).\n- 3.1 (V1 p761): verify insulation before closing.\n\n5. 079219 ACOUSTICAL JOINT SEALANTS (V1 p562-566)\n- Latex sealant \"ASTM C834\".\n- \"STC-Rated Assemblies: Seal construction at perimeters, behind control joints, and at openings and penetrations with a continuous bead of acoustical sealant ... both faces ... ASTM C919, ASTM C1193\" (summary with verbatim fragments).\n\n6. 092116.23\n- 2.03.I (V1 p741): \"Insulation: Sound attenuation blankets.\"\n- 3.03.H (V1 p743): \"Sound-Rated Shaft Wall Assemblies: Seal gypsum board shaft walls with acoustical sealant at perimeter of each assembly where it abuts other work and at joints and penetrations within each assembly.\"\n\n7. NOT FOUND\n- 072100 (V1 p426) and 092116.23 (V1 p742) refer to 092900 for \"sound attenuation blanket\", but 092900 does not specify it: NOT FOUND.\n- Acoustic tests/design (STC test number) per type: \"TESTED ASSEMBLIES - SOUND\" column empty in A8.1.\n"},"00_indice_fuentes.txt":{"titulo":"INDEX AND SOURCES - DRYWALL INSTALLATION SPECIFICATIONS","texto":"INDEX AND SOURCES - DRYWALL INSTALLATION SPECIFICATIONS\n(read only; verbatim English phrases in quotes; page = PDF page)\n\nSOURCE ABBREVIATIONS\n- V1    = Project Manual Volume 1 (Div. 1-14)\n- V2    = Project Manual Volume 2 (Div. 14-28, MEP) -> no drywall sections\n- A2    = Addendum 2 Specs (reissues 014339, 092900, 098100)\n- A3    = Addendum 3 Project Manual (reissues 054000 and 072100; does not reissue Div. 09 drywall)\n- RFI-092900 = Spec 092900 Gypsum Board revised by RFI-092900, 13 pp. (most recent version of 092900)\n- A8.1  = Sheet A8.1 Partition Types and Schedules, revision RFI-A8.1\n- A3.21 = Sheet A3.21 Ceiling Details, IFC (arrived later; see 16_detalles_plafon_A3.21.txt)\n\nSECTION LOCATIONS\n- 092116.23 Gypsum Board Shaft Wall Assemblies ... V1 p739-744\n- 092216 Non-Structural Metal Framing ............ V1 p745-754\n- 092900 Gypsum Board ............................ V1 p755-765 / A2 p145-155 / RFI-092900 p1-13\n- 098100 Acoustic Insulation ..................... V1 p828-832 / A2 p156-161\n- 078400 Firestopping ............................ V1 p535-544\n- 078443 Joint Firestopping ...................... V1 p545-549\n- 079219 Acoustical Joint Sealants ............... V1 p562-566\n- 072100 Thermal Insulation ...................... V1 p425-431 / A3 p227-235\n- 054000 Cold-Formed Metal Framing ............... V1 p282+ / A3 p169-181\n- 014339 Mockups ................................. V1 p68-70 / A2 p5-7\n- \"092116\" (gypsum board assemblies, without .23) ... NOT FOUND as its own section\n\nFILES IN THIS FOLDER (created by this task)\n01_patron_tornillos.txt\n02_ubicacion_paredes_planos.txt\n03_normas_GA216_ASTM.txt\n04_bastidor_metalico_092216.txt\n05_RC1_resilient_channel.txt\n06_shaft_wall.txt\n07_fire_rated_2h_UL_GA.txt\n08_acabado_niveles_tolerancias.txt\n09_juntas_de_control.txt\n10_firestopping_fire_caulking.txt\n11_deflection_head_of_wall.txt\n12_aislamiento_acustico.txt\n13_blocking_refuerzos.txt\n14_submittals_mockups.txt\n15_placa_yeso_materiales_instalacion.txt\n16_detalles_plafon_A3.21.txt\n(paredes_2h_fire_rated.txt already existed; this task did not create or modify it)\n\nMAIN MISSING ITEMS (see each file)\n- Edge/field screw pattern per layer: NOT FOUND (refers to UL / GA-600 / GA-216 / C840)\n- UL/GA design numbers for rated walls: NOT FOUND (blank columns in A8.1; \"UL ###\" at concrete)\n- Term \"RC1\" / \"RC-1\" for resilient channel: NOT FOUND (RC-1 in V2 p113 is \"Roof Curb, Type 1\")\n- Floor plans with the location of each wall type: NOT FOUND (no plans in the folder)\n- Head-of-wall / top-of-wall details (\"T.O. WALL DETAIL\" columns in A8.1 blank): NOT FOUND\n"},"01_patron_tornillos.txt":{"titulo":"SCREW PATTERN AND SPACING","texto":"SCREW PATTERN AND SPACING\n\n1. SCREW TYPE (092900 2.3.C; V1 p760 / RFI-092900 p6)\n- \"ASTM C1002, Type S or Type A; bugle head; self drilling and self tapping screws for light gauge steel framing (less than 0.033 inch thick).\"\n- \"ASTM C954; bugle head; self-drilling and self tapping screws for heavy gauge steel framing (0.033 inch to 0.112 inch thick).\"\n- \"ASTM C1002 Type W or Type A; bugle head; provide sufficient length to provide a minimum 3/4 inch penetration into wood framing members.\"\n- Sound damping board (092900 2.2.E.2.b; V1 p760 / RFI-092900 p6): \"ASTM C1002 Type S or S12 fine thread drywall screws.\"\n- Shaft wall (092116.23 2.06.C; V1 p742): \"Steel Drill Screws: ASTM C 1002 unless otherwise indicated.\"\n- Cement board (092900 2.5.B.1; V1 p761 / RFI-092900 p7): \"Screws: No. 6 gauge by sufficient length to penetrate 3/4 inch into wood framing and 3/8 inch into steel framing, self-drilling, ribbed wafer head or bugle head screws; minimum 500 hour corrosion resistant finish per ASTM B117.\"\n\n2. SPACING FOUND\n- General rule (092900 3.3.F; V1 p762 / RFI-092900 p8): \"Use screws to fasten gypsum board to framing.\"\n- Rated walls (092900 3.4.A; V1 p763 / RFI-092900 p9): \"Install fire rated assemblies using materials, application methods including types and spacing of fasteners, and framing in accordance with the specified UL Fire Resistive Design Number, GA-600 File Number, or CBC Table 720.\"\n- Sound damping (092900 3.5.D; V1 p763 / RFI-092900 p9): \"Secure gypsum board to metal framing using fine thread drywall screws at 16 inches on center, minimum.\"\n- Cement board (092900 3.7.D; V1 p764 / RFI-092900 p10): \"Space fasteners maximum 8 inches on center with perimeter fasteners at least 3/8 inch from ends and 5/8 inch from edges.\"\n- Cement board (092900 3.7.E): \"Drive screws so bottoms of heads are flush with surface of boards ... Do not overdrive screws and replace any screws that are stripped.\"\n- Gypsum sheathing (092900 3.6.D; V1 p763 / RFI-092900 p10): \"Do not countersink fasteners. Fasteners must be located at least 3/8 inches from edges and ends of sheathing panels.\"\n- Cant panels at shaft (092116.23 3.03.I.1; V1 p743): \"secure top edges to shaft walls at 24 inches o.c. with screws fastened to shaft wall framing.\"\n- Finishing (092900 3.8.C; V1 p764): \"Set mechanical fasteners below the plane of the board.\"\n\n- A3.21 detalle 2E: \"2 1/4\" DIA SS SELF TAPPING SCREW @ 12\" O.C. - VERIFY W/ MANUF.\" (at header / H.M. door frame connection, not board).\n\n3. NOT FOUND\n- Screw spacing at EDGE and in FIELD for standard / Type X gypsum board: NOT FOUND.\n- Spacing per LAYER (1st layer / face layer) in multilayer systems: NOT FOUND.\n- Specific screw length (1\", 1-5/8\", etc.) for board on steel: NOT FOUND\n  (only minimum penetration 3/4\" in wood and 3/8\" in steel for cement board).\n- Minimum screw distance to edge for gypsum board (not sheathing or cement board): NOT FOUND.\n- What is missing: UL/GA design number of each wall (to read the screw pattern from the listing) or GA-216 / ASTM C840 table (external documents, not included).\n"},"02_ubicacion_paredes_planos.txt":{"titulo":"WALL LOCATIONS / PARTITION TYPES (SHEET A8.1, rev. RFI-A8.1","texto":"WALL LOCATIONS / PARTITION TYPES (SHEET A8.1, rev. RFI-A8.1\n\n1. GENERAL NOTES (A8.1, General Notes; verbatim transcription)\n- N1: \"ALL STANDARD STUD FRAMING SHALL BE 16\" O.C. UNO; ALL SHAFT WALL STUD FRAMING SHALL BE 24\" O.C. UNO.\"\n- N2: \"FIRE RATING AS INDICATED ON CODE PLANS.\"\n- N3: \"ALL GYP. BD. TO BE 5/8\" THICK TYPE 'X' UNO; PAINT ALL EXPOSED FACES.\"\n- N4: \"PROVIDE TYPE 'X' GLASS-MAT WATER-RESISTANT BACKER BOARD IN LIEU OF FINAL LAYER OF GYP.BD. WHERE INDICATED ON SCHEDULED WALL TYPES AT INTERIOR FRAMED WET WALLS, INCLUDING SHOWER ENCLOSURES, JANITOR'S CLOSETS, AND AT NON-FIRE RATED AND NON-ACOUSTICAL WALLS AT BATHTUB ENCLOSURES.\"\n- N5: \"PROVIDE MOISTURE AND MOLD-RESISTANT TYPE 'X' GYP. BD. AT ALL INTERIOR \"HUMID\" LOCATIONS INCLUDING BATHROOMS\"\n- N6: \"THE WALL TYPE ABOVE OR BELOW ANY OPENING IS TO BE THE SAME AS THAT SCHEDULED FOR EITHER SIDE OF THE OPENING\"\n- N7: \"DIFFERING WALL TYPES SHALL ALIGN SO THAT WALL PLANES CONTINUE UNBROKEN IN ROOMS, U.O.N.\"\n- N8: \"DIMENSION LOCATION FOR ALL INTERIOR WALLS IS TO THE FACE OF GYPSUM BOARD PANELS. WALLS INDICATED AS FIRE-RATED FORM A SEPARATION THAT SHALL BE CONTINUOUS FROM FLOOR TO FLOOR ABOVE WITH NO BREAKS AT COLUMNS, BEAMS, WALL TRANSITIONS, OR OTHER OBSTRUCTIONS. AT RATED CONDITIONS PENETRATIONS SHALL BE FIRE CAULKED.\"\n- N10: \"STUDS TO BE 25 GA AT ALL SINGLE-STUD ACOUSTICAL WALLS U.O.N. USE KNURLED STUDS OR ADJUST SPACING AS REQUIRED FOR SPANS. SPACING SHALL BE NO LESS THAN 16\" O.C. WITHOUT APPROVAL OF THE ARCHITECT. HEAVIER GAUGE MAY BE USED AT NON-ACOUSTICAL OR DOUBLE-STUD WALLS. MAXIMUM SPANS SHALL BE BASED ON L/240 DEFLECTION CRITERIA AND 5 PSF DESIGN LOAD, U.O.N.\"\n- N13: \"WALL SUPPORTING CASEWORK, SHELVING, GRAB BARS, EQUIPMENT AND OTHER WALL-MOUNTED FIXTURES SHALL BE RINFORCED PER THE FIXTURE MANUFACTURER'S REQUIREMENTS AND THE REQUIREMENTS OF THE LATEST EDITION OF THE [STATE BUILDING CODE], WHICHEVER IS GREATER.\"\n- (N9, N11, N12 in 12_aislamiento_acustico.txt and 10_firestopping_fire_caulking.txt)\n\n2. HOW TO READ THE TAG (A8.1 \"Partition Type Descriptions\", e.g. SA.3.10)\n- Material: S = steel (metal studs); W = wood studs; H = shaft walls; M = masonry; C = concrete\n- Rating/height: A = full height/underside of struct (not rated); B = 6\" above ceiling; C = underside of ceiling; D = partial height; S = smoke partition; Y = variable height; 0 = 0 hour (corridor); 5 = 0.5 h; 1 = 1 h; 2 = 2 h; 3 = 3 h; 4 = 4 h\n- Width: 1 = 1 5/8\" metal; 2 = 2 1/2\" metal; 3 = 3 5/8\" metal; 4 = 4\" metal; 6 = 6\" metal; 8 = 8\" metal; 10/12 concrete/CMU; D = double stud; S = staggered stud; Y = non-standard; F = furring strips/channels\n- GWB indicator (\"NOT INCLUDING SHAFT LINER\"): 0 = no GWB; 1 = one layer, tag side; 2 = one layer, each side; 3 = two layers tag side, one layer other; 4 = two layers, each side; 5 = two layers tag side, no GWB other; 6 = three layers, each side\n- Unique condition: 0 = none; 1 = load bearing [stud]; 2 = sound attenuation blanket; 3 = resilient channels w/ sound attenuation blanket; 4 = tile backer board to receive tile finish; 5 = [concrete] wall w/ corrugated metal formwork; 6 = typical framing @ underside of joist; 7 = [concrete] wall w/ corrugated metal formwork and resilient channel w/ GWB finish on stair side; 8 = tile backer board w/ air and vapor barrier\n\n3. TYPES SCHEDULED IN A8.1 (mark / overall thickness / rating / STC / comment)\n- SA-X-1X non-rated: SA-1-10, SA-1-16, SA-3-10, SA-3-12 (4 1/4\"), SA-3-18 (4 1/4\"), SA-6-10 / SA-6-12 / SA-6-18 (6 5/8\") - NR\n- SA-X-2X non-rated: SA-3-20 / SA-3-22 / SA-3-28 (4 7/8\"), SA-6-20 / SA-6-22 / SA-6-28 (7 1/4\"), SD-3-20 (4 7/8\") - NR\n- S1-X-2X 1 hour: S1-3-10 (4 1/4\"), S1-3-20 (4 7/8\"), S1-3-23 (11 3/4\", STC 64, \"RESIDENTIAL / FITNESS SEPARATION\"), S1-6-13 (7 1/8\", STC 56, \"TYPICAL CORRIDOR / DEMISING\"), S1-6-20 (7 1/4\"), S1-6-22 (7 1/4\"), S1-6-23 (7 3/4\", STC 56, \"TYPICAL CORRIDOR / DEMISING\"), S1-6-42 (8 1/2\"), S1-8-20 (9 1/4\")\n- S2-X-4X 2 hours: S2-3-40 (6 1/8\"), S2-6-40 (8 1/2\"), S2-6-41 (8 1/2\") - rating 2\n- H2-X-3X 2 hours shaft: H2-4-30 (6 1/4\"), H2-6-30 (7 1/4\") - rating 2; drawing: \"C-H METAL STUD FRAMING\", \"1\" GYPSUM SHAFT LINER\"\n- Masonry: M0-8-00, M1-8-00, M2-8-00, M4-8-00 (7 5/8\")\n- Concrete: CA-6-00, CA-12-00 (NR); C2-6-00, C2-8-00, C2-10-00, C2-10-07, C2-11-05, C2-11-07, C2-12-00, C2-18-00 (rating 2)\n- Details on the sheet: \"DETAIL AT SHAFT WALLS\", \"ELEVATOR SHAFT DETAIL\", \"ELEVATOR SHAFT DETAIL @ SHEAR WALL\" (scale 1 1/2\" = 1'-0\"); note in shaft detail: \"NO LIMITING HEIGHT IF BRACED TO ADJACENT CONSTRUCTION\"; \"FIRE PROTECTION SPRINKLER HEAD @ ALTERNATING FLOORS AND TOP OF SHAFT, TYP.\"\n\n4. NOT FOUND\n- Plans / floor plans with the location of each wall type: NOT FOUND (only A8.1 exists in the folder; also N2 refers to \"CODE PLANS\", not included).\n- Columns \"TESTED ASSEMBLIES\", \"T.O. WALL DETAIL\", \"B.O. WALL DETAIL\": empty (blank yellow cells) in the stud and shaft tables.\n- A8.1 sheet notes are not in spec 092900/092216 (e.g. 25 GA, 24\" o.c. shaft) -> only in A8.1.\n"},"03_normas_GA216_ASTM.txt":{"titulo":"REFERENCED STANDARDS (GA, ASTM, UL, CBC/[state code])","texto":"REFERENCED STANDARDS (GA, ASTM, UL, CBC/[state code])\n\n1. 092900 GYPSUM BOARD - 1.2 REFERENCES (V1 p756-757 / RFI-092900 p2-3)\n- \"ASTM C840 - Standard Specification for Application and Finishing of Gypsum Board.\"\n- \"ASTM C954 - Standard Specification for Steel Drill Screws ... to Steel Studs From 0.033 in. (0.84 mm) to 0.112 in. (2.84 mm) in Thickness.\"\n- \"ASTM C1002 - Standard Specification for Steel Self-Piercing Tapping Screws for the Application of Gypsum Panel Products or Metal Plaster Bases to Wood Studs or Steel Studs.\"\n- \"ASTM C1047 - Standard Specification for Accessories for Gypsum Wallboard and Gypsum Veneer Base.\"\n- \"ASTM C1396/C1396M - Standard Specification for Gypsum Board.\"\n- \"ASTM C475/C475M\" (joint compound/tape); C473; C1177; C1278; C1325; D3273; E695; B117; D226; D1117; D2103; E96; ANSI A108.11, A118.1, A118.4, A118.9\n- \"GA-214 - Recommended Levels of Gypsum Board Finish.\"\n- \"GA-216 - Application and Finishing of Gypsum Board.\"\n- \"GA-253 - Application of Gypsum Sheathing.\"\n- \"GA-600 - Fire Resistance Design Manual.\"\n- \"UL Fire Resistance Directory.\"\n- 1.4.B (V1 p757): \"Regulatory Requirements: Comply with requirements of CBC Chapter 25.\"\n\n2. WHERE THEY APPLY IN 092900\n- 3.3.A (V1 p762 / RFI-092900 p8): \"Install gypsum board to framing and furring members in accordance with manufacturer's recommendations, GA-216 or ASTM C840, and as specified in this Section.\"\n- 3.4.A (V1 p763 / RFI-092900 p9): \"... in accordance with the specified UL Fire Resistive Design Number, GA-600 File Number, or CBC Table 720.\"\n- 3.5.A (V1 p763): \"Install sound damping gypsum board in accordance with GA-201, GA-216, GA-600, and manufacturer's instructions.\"\n- 3.5.C (V1 p763): \"Comply with ASTM C919 ...\"\n- 3.6.A: \"Install gypsum sheathing in accordance with GA-253 and manufacturer's instructions.\"\n- 3.8.A (V1 p764 / RFI-092900 p10): \"Finish gypsum board surfaces in accordance with ASTM C840, GA-214 and GA-216.\"\n- RFI-092900 3.8.G (p10): \"Finish gypsum board in accordance with levels defined in ASTM C840, as follows:\"\n\n3. 092216 NON-STRUCTURAL METAL FRAMING\n- 2.03.A (V1 p747): \"Framing Members, General: Comply with ASTM C 754 for conditions indicated.\"\n- 2.03.A.1-2: \"Comply with ASTM C 645 requirements for metal\" ; \"Protective Coating: ASTM A 653/A 653M, G40, hot-dip galvanized unless otherwise indicated.\"\n- 3.03.A (V1 p751): \"Installation Standard: ASTM C 754.\" / \"Gypsum Board Assemblies: Also comply with requirements in ASTM C 840 that apply to framing installation.\"\n- 2.02.A-B (V1 p746): fire \"ASTM E 119\"; STC \"ASTM E 90 and classified according to ASTM E 413\".\n- 1.03.B.1 (V1 p746): SIFA certification \"Code Compliance Certification Program for Cold-Formed Steel Structural and Non-Structural Framing Members.\"\n- Firestop top track seal (V1 p749): \"UL 2079 tested\"; ASTM E84; ASTM G21.\n\n4. 092116.23 SHAFT WALL\n- 1.06.A (V1 p740): \"Comply with ASTM C 840 requirements or with gypsum board manufacturer's written recommendations, whichever are more stringent.\"\n- 3.03.A (V1 p743): \"... manufacturer's written installation instructions, and ASTM C 754 other than stud-spacing requirements.\"\n- 2.04 (V1 p741): shaftliner \"ASTM C 1396/C 1396M\" and \"ASTM C 1658/C 1658M\"; mold \"ASTM D 3273 ... ASTM D 3274\".\n- 2.06.C (V1 p742): \"ASTM C 1002\"; anchors ASTM E 488 / ASTM E 1190.\n\n5. OTHERS\n- 078400 (V1 p538-539): ASTM E2307, ASTM E2837, ASTM E1966, UL 2079, ASTM E814, UL 1479; inspection ASTM E 2174 / ASTM E 2393 (p542).\n- 078443 (V1 p547-549): ASTM E1966 or UL 2079; ASTM E84; ASTM E2393.\n- 079219 (V1 p564-565): ASTM E90, ASTM C834, ASTM C919, ASTM C1193.\n- A8.1 N12: \"[state code] 2015, SECTION 706.\" ; N13: \"[STATE BUILDING CODE]\".\n- Observation (not interpreted): 092900 cites \"CBC Chapter 25\" and \"CBC Table 720\"; A8.1 cites [state code] 2015 / [state building code].\n\n6. NOT FOUND\n- Text of GA-216, GA-600, ASTM C840, C754 (external documents; not in the folder).\n- Specific GA-600 \"File Number\" for each wall: NOT FOUND.\n"},"06_shaft_wall.txt":{"titulo":"SHAFT WALL - SECTION 092116.23 GYPSUM BOARD SHAFT WALL ASSEMBLIES (V1 p739-744) + A8.1","texto":"SHAFT WALL - SECTION 092116.23 GYPSUM BOARD SHAFT WALL ASSEMBLIES (V1 p739-744) + A8.1\n\n1. ASSEMBLY (2.03, p740-741)\n- \"Fire-Resistance Rating: As indicated.\" / \"STC Rating: As indicated.\"\n- Studs: \"Manufacturer's standard profile for repetitive members, corner and end members, and fire-resistance-rated assembly indicated.\" ; \"Depth: As indicated.\" ; \"Minimum Base-Metal Thickness: 0.018 inch.\"\n- Runner: \"Manufacturer's standard J-profile track with manufacturer's standard long-leg length, but at least 2 inches long and matching studs in depth.\"\n- \"Firestop Tracks: Provide firestop track at head of shaft wall on each floor level.\"\n- Elevator: \"J-profile jamb strut with long-leg length of 3 inches, matching studs in depth, and not less than 0.033 inch thick.\"\n- \"Room-Side Finish: Gypsum board.\"\n- Shaft-side: \"Typical: Gypsum shaftliner board, moisture- and mold-resistant Type X.\" / \"Extended Outdoor Exposure: Gypsum shaftliner board, extended exposure Type X.\"\n- \"Insulation: Sound attenuation blankets.\"\n- Shaftliner (2.04.B-C, p741): \"Thickness: 1 inch.\" ; \"Long Edges: Double bevel.\" ; \"Mold Resistance: ASTM D 3273, score of 10\"; extended exposure: \"Able to withstand up to 12 months of outdoor weather exposure.\"\n- Screws (2.06.C, p742): \"Steel Drill Screws: ASTM C 1002 unless otherwise indicated.\"\n- Acoustic (2.06.F): \"Acoustical Sealant: As specified in Section 079219\".\n\n2. INSTALLATION (3.03, p742-743)\n- \"Install gypsum board shaft wall assemblies to comply with requirements of fire-resistance-rated assemblies indicated, manufacturer's written installation instructions, and ASTM C 754 other than stud-spacing requirements.\"\n- \"Do not bridge building expansion joints with shaft wall assemblies; frame both sides of expansion joints with furring and other support.\"\n- \"Elevator Hoistway: At elevator hoistway-entrance door frames, provide jamb struts on each side of door frame.\"\n- Handrails: \"provide galvanized steel reinforcing strip with 0.033-inch minimum thickness of base metal (uncoated), accurately positioned and secured behind at least one layer of face panel.\"\n- Penetrations: \"maintain fire-resistance rating ... by installing supplementary steel framing around perimeter of penetration and fire protection behind boxes containing wiring devices, elevator call buttons, elevator floor indicators, and similar items.\"\n- \"Isolate perimeter of gypsum panels from building structure to prevent cracking of panels, while maintaining continuity of fire-rated construction.\"\n- \"Firestop Tracks: Where indicated, install to maintain continuity of fire-resistance-rated assembly indicated.\"\n- \"Control Joints: Install control joints according to ASTM C 840 and in specific locations approved by Architect while maintaining fire-resistance rating\"\n- \"Sound-Rated Shaft Wall Assemblies: Seal gypsum board shaft walls with acoustical sealant at perimeter of each assembly where it abuts other work and at joints and penetrations within each assembly.\"\n- Cant panels: \"At projections into shaft exceeding 4 inches ... install 1/2- or 5/8-inch- thick gypsum board cants\" ; \"Slope cant panels at least 75 degrees from horizontal\" ; \"secure top edges to shaft walls at 24 inches o.c. with screws\" ; support framing \"at 24 inches o.c.\"\n- \"Installation Tolerance: Install each framing member so fastening surfaces vary not more than 1/8 inch from the plane formed by faces of adjacent framing.\"\n- Fireproofing (3.02): do not reduce SFRM thickness \"below that which is required to obtain fire-resistance rating indicated.\"\n- Shaft deflection (092216 2.02.C, V1 p746): \"Shaft Walls at HVAC Shafts: 10 lbf/sq. ft.\" / \"Shaft Walls at Elevator Shafts: 10 lbf/sq. ft.\"\n\n3. A8.1\n- N1: \"ALL SHAFT WALL STUD FRAMING SHALL BE 24\" O.C. UNO.\"\n- H2-X-3X 2-HOUR RATED SHAFT PARTITIONS: H2-4-30 (6 1/4\"), H2-6-30 (7 1/4\"), rating 2.\n- GWB indicator 3: \"TWO LAYERS TAG SIDE, ONE LAYER OTHER\" (does not include shaft liner); drawing: \"C-H METAL STUD FRAMING\", \"1\" GYPSUM SHAFT LINER\", sound attenuation blanket.\n- N11.g: acoustic walls include \"SHAFT WALLS AT UNITS\".\n- Details: \"DETAIL AT SHAFT WALLS\", \"ELEVATOR SHAFT DETAIL\", \"ELEVATOR SHAFT DETAIL @ SHEAR WALL\"; note \"NO LIMITING HEIGHT IF BRACED TO ADJACENT CONSTRUCTION\".\n\n4. NOT FOUND\n- UL/GA design of shaft H2 (\"TESTED ASSEMBLIES\" column blank): NOT FOUND.\n- Screw pattern/spacing per layer at shaft: NOT FOUND.\n- Maximum height / gauge by height of C-H studs: NOT FOUND.\n- Shop drawing submittal for shaft: NOT FOUND (only product data and ICC-ES).\n"},"07_fire_rated_2h_UL_GA.txt":{"titulo":"FIRE-RATED WALLS (1 h / 2 h) - UL / GA / CBC","texto":"FIRE-RATED WALLS (1 h / 2 h) - UL / GA / CBC\n\n1. GENERAL REQUIREMENT\n- 092900 3.4.A (V1 p763 / RFI-092900 p9): \"Install fire rated assemblies using materials, application methods including types and spacing of fasteners, and framing in accordance with the specified UL Fire Resistive Design Number, GA-600 File Number, or CBC Table 720.\"\n- 092900 3.4.B: \"Completely seal joints of fire-rated gypsum board enclosures in accordance with UL or GA listed assembly requirements. Seal penetrations through rated partitions and ceilings in accordance with tested systems. Refer to Section 078400.\"\n- 092216 2.02.A (V1 p746): \"provide materials and construction identical to those tested in assembly indicated, according to ASTM E 119 by an independent testing agency.\"\n- 092216 3.04.D.4 (V1 p752): \"Fire-Resistance-Rated Partitions: Install framing to comply with fire-resistance-rated assembly indicated and support closures and to make partitions continuous from floor to underside of solid structure.\"\n- 092216 3.05.D (V1 p754): \"Fire-Resistance-Rated Assemblies: Wire tie furring channels to supports.\"\n- 092116.23 2.02.A (V1 p740): \"provide materials and construction identical to those tested in assembly indicated according to ASTM E 119\".\n- 092900 3.2.A (V1 p761 / RFI-092900 p8): \"Install intumescent moldable pads over backs and sides of all electrical junction and utility boxes at fire rated walls.\"\n- 092900 2.3.B: control joints \"provided in accordance with UL fire rated assemblies.\"\n\n2. BOARD\n- 092900 2.2.A: \"Type X: ASTM C1396/1396M; 5/8-inch thick; fire resistant core\"\n- A8.1 N3: \"ALL GYP. BD. TO BE 5/8\" THICK TYPE 'X' UNO\"\n- RFI-092900 3.3.A.1 (p8): \"Install type X gypsum board at corridors.\" (V1 p762 and A2 p152 said \"Install hi-impact gypsum board at corridors.\")\n\n3. RATED TYPES IN A8.1\n- 1 h: S1-3-10, S1-3-20, S1-3-23, S1-6-13, S1-6-20, S1-6-22, S1-6-23, S1-6-42, S1-8-20; M1-8-00\n- 2 h studs: S2-3-40 (6 1/8\"), S2-6-40 (8 1/2\"), S2-6-41 (8 1/2\") -> indicator 4 = \"TWO LAYERS, EACH SIDE\"; modifier 1 on S2-6-41 = load bearing stud\n- 2 h shaft: H2-4-30 (6 1/4\"), H2-6-30 (7 1/4\") -> indicator 3 = \"TWO LAYERS TAG SIDE, ONE LAYER OTHER\" + 1\" shaft liner\n- 2 h masonry/concrete: M2-8-00; C2-6-00 ... C2-18-00\n- 4 h: M4-8-00\n- A8.1 N8: \"WALLS INDICATED AS FIRE-RATED FORM A SEPARATION THAT SHALL BE CONTINUOUS FROM FLOOR TO FLOOR ABOVE WITH NO BREAKS AT COLUMNS, BEAMS, WALL TRANSITIONS, OR OTHER OBSTRUCTIONS. AT RATED CONDITIONS PENETRATIONS SHALL BE FIRE CAULKED.\"\n- A8.1 N2: \"FIRE RATING AS INDICATED ON CODE PLANS.\"\n- A8.1 legend: \"SPRAY APPLIED FIREPROOFING (UL VERIFICATION BY COLD-FORMED METAL FRAMING SUBCONTRACTOR\"\n\n4. DESIGN NUMBERS\n- UL design (U4xx, V4xx, W4xx, etc.): NOT FOUND in any source.\n- GA-600 File Number: NOT FOUND.\n- CBC Table 720 item: NOT FOUND.\n- A8.1: \"TESTED ASSEMBLIES\" columns empty for S1, S2, H2; some C2 concrete rows show only the marker \"UL ###\" (no number).\n\n5. NOT FOUND / WHAT IS MISSING\n- Code drawings (\"CODE PLANS\") with the location of rated walls: not in the folder.\n- Screw pattern per layer for 2 h: depends on the UL/GA design, not indicated.\n"},"09_juntas_de_control.txt":{"titulo":"CONTROL JOINTS","texto":"CONTROL JOINTS\n\n1. PRODUCT (092900 2.3.B; V1 p760 / RFI-092900 p6)\n- \"Control Joints: ASTM C1047; roll-formed zinc joint with removable protected opening; provided in accordance with UL fire rated assemblies.\" (manufacturer product omitted)\n\n2. MINIMUM LOCATIONS (092900 3.3.I; V1 p762 / RFI-092900 p8-9)\n- \"Install consistent with lines of building spaces and as a minimum, install as follows:\"\n- \"Where a partition, wall, or ceiling traverses a construction joint (expansion, seismic, or building control element) in the base building structure.\"\n- \"Where a wall or partition runs in an uninterrupted straight plane exceeding 30 linear feet.\"\n- \"In interior ceilings with perimeter relief so that linear dimensions between control joints do not exceed 50 feet and total area between control joints does not exceed 2500 square feet.\"\n- \"In interior ceilings without perimeter relief so that linear dimensions between control joints do not exceed 30 feet and total area between control joints does not exceed 900 square feet.\"\n- \"In exterior ceilings and soffits so that linear dimensions between control joints do not exceed 30 feet and total area between control joints do not exceed 900 square feet.\"\n- \"Where ceiling framing members change direction.\"\n- \"Where a partition transitions from floor-supported framing to overhead hung framing.\"\n\n3. RELATED\n- 092216 3.03.E (V1 p751): \"Do not bridge building control and expansion joints with non-load-bearing steel framing members. Frame both sides of joints independently.\"\n- 092216 3.04.D.2.b (V1 p752): cripple studs \"with a minimum 1/2-inch clearance from jamb stud to allow for installation of control joint in finished assembly.\"\n- 092116.23 3.03.G (V1 p743): \"Install control joints according to ASTM C 840 and in specific locations approved by Architect while maintaining fire-resistance rating of gypsum board shaft wall assemblies.\"\n- 092900 3.5.C: seal \"behind control joints\" with acoustic sealant.\n\n- A3.21 (ceilings): details 1A, 1B \"CONTROL JOINT\"; 1C \"1\" EJ\" + \"CONT EXP JT COVER\"; 2A/2B \"SUSPEND GYPSUM CEILING CONTROL JOINT\" with \"4\" MAX.\" and \"1/4\"\". See 16_detalles_plafon_A3.21.txt.\n\n4. NOT FOUND\n- Location of control joints on plans/elevations: NOT FOUND.\n- Control joint detail at rated wall (backing, firestop behind the joint): NOT FOUND.\n"},"10_firestopping_fire_caulking.txt":{"titulo":"FIRESTOPPING / FIRE CAULKING / JOINT SYSTEMS","texto":"FIRESTOPPING / FIRE CAULKING / JOINT SYSTEMS\n\n1. A8.1\n- N8: \"AT RATED CONDITIONS PENETRATIONS SHALL BE FIRE CAULKED.\"\n- N12: \"PROVIDE FIRE-RESISTIVE JOINT SYSTEM BETWEEN FIRE-RESISTIVE WALLS AND FIRE-RESISTIVE FLOORS OR FIRE-RESISTIVE CEILINGS PER REQUIREMENTS OF [state code] 2015, SECTION 706.\"\n\n2. 092900\n- 3.1.F (V1 p761 / RFI-092900 p8): \"Verify firestopping work, refer to Section 078400.\"\n- 3.2.A: \"Install intumescent moldable pads over backs and sides of all electrical junction and utility boxes at fire rated walls.\"\n- 2.3.H (V1 p760): putty pads \"7.25 inches by 7.25 inches\" or \"9 inches by 9 inches\" (product omitted).\n- 3.4.B (V1 p763): \"Completely seal joints of fire-rated gypsum board enclosures in accordance with UL or GA listed assembly requirements.\"\n\n3. 092216 (V1 p748-749)\n- Firestop top track seal: \"One-piece, pre-formed, polyurethane foam based, firestop seal for use with standard head-joint top tracks and bottom-joint tracks, and slip-type head joints in fire-rated construction\"\n- \"Use only firestop top track seal product that has been UL 2079 tested for specific fire-rated construction conditions\"\n- \"Movement: +/- 50 percent.\" ; ASTM E84 \"Flame Spread: 10; Smoke Development: 20\" ; ASTM G21 \"Class 0\" ; \"VOC Content: 0.18 lb./gallon maximum.\"\n- 3.04.D.4.b (p752): \"Firestop Track Seals: Install to maintain continuity of fire-resistance-rated assembly indicated. Install in accordance with the firestop manufacturer's printed installation instructions.\"\n\n4. 078400 FIRESTOPPING (V1 p535-544)\n- 1.02.A.4: includes \"Gaps between the top of walls and ceilings or roof assemblies.\"\n- 2.03.B (p538): \"Head-of-Wall Firestopping at Joints Between Non-Rated Floor and Fire-Rated Wall: Use any system that has been tested according to ASTM E2837 to have fire resistance F Rating equal to required fire rating of floor or wall, whichever is greater.\"\n- 2.03.C: joints where both are rated: \"tested according to ASTM E1966 or UL 2079\"\n- 2.03.D: penetrations: \"ASTM E814 or UL 1479\"\n- 2.03.F (p539): joints in smoke barriers \"L Rating not exceeding 5.0 cfm/linear. ft.\"\n- 1.04.C (p536): submittal \"Schedule of Firestopping ... design designation of qualified testing and inspecting agency\"; engineering judgments with AHJ approval.\n- 3.03.D (p541): \"Do not cover installed firestopping until inspected by field quality control service and authority having jurisdiction.\"\n- 3.04.A (p541): label walls \"FIRE AND/OR SMOKE BARRIER - PROTECT ALL OPENINGS,\" letters \"not less than 3 inches high\"; \"at 15 feet from end of wall and at intervals not exceeding 30 feet.\"\n- 3.05.A (p542): inspection \"ASTM E 2174 ... and ASTM E 2393\"\n- 3.08 (p542-544): table of UL systems; \"HW\" = Head of Wall; \"SYSTEMS PROVIDED BELOW ARE FOR GUIDANCE ONLY\".\n\n5. 078443 JOINT FIRESTOPPING (V1 p545-549)\n- 2.2.B (p547): \"Provide joint firestopping systems with ratings determined per ASTM E1966 or UL 2079.\"\n- 2.2.B.2 (p548): \"Fire-Resistance Rating: Equal to or exceeding the fire-resistance rating of the wall, floor, or roof in or between which it is installed.\"\n- 1.6.A (p546): approved installer FM 4991 or UL Qualified Firestop Contractor.\n- 3.4.A (p549): inspection \"according to ASTM E2393.\"\n\n6. NOT FOUND\n- Specific UL system number (HW-D-xxxx, W-L-xxxx) per condition of this project: NOT FOUND.\n- Graphic details of rated head-of-wall: NOT FOUND.\n"},"14_submittals_mockups.txt":{"titulo":"SUBMITTALS AND MOCK-UPS","texto":"SUBMITTALS AND MOCK-UPS\n\n1. 092900 (V1 p757 / RFI-092900 p3)\n- Product data.\n- \"Submit two 2 foot by 2 foot samples of spray-applied texture finish.\"\n- Installer: experience of 5 projects in 3 years (summary).\n- \"Comply with requirements of CBC Chapter 25.\"\n\n2. 092216 (V1 p746)\n- Product data; SIFA certification; firestop tracks and seals; smoke/acoustic seals.\n- Delegated design: \"to frame openings over 4 feet in width ... structural calculations ... signed, and sealed\" (fragments).\n- Evaluation reports ICC-ES.\n\n3. 092116.23 (V1 p740)\n- Product data; ICC-ES reports for shaft walls and firestop tracks.\n\n4. 078400 / 078443 (V1 p536, p546)\n- Product data; schedule with UL designation; engineering judgments approved by AHJ; test reports; compatibility; installer certificates; schedule of installed firestopping.\n\n5. 098100 (A2 p156)\n- Product data and \"installation procedures\".\n\n6. 079219 (V1 p562-563)\n- Product data and samples.\n\n7. MOCK-UPS - 014339 (A2 p5-7)\n- \"(1) standard and (1) ADA Type A Unit on the lowest residential floors\"\n- \"Mockup to be complete and ready for review, no later than 2 months prior to material installation\"\n- Notice 7 days before; 7-day review.\n- \"See mockup details on Architectural Drawings.\"\n\n8. NOT FOUND\n- Specific drywall/framing/finishing mock-up in 092216, 092900 or 092116.23: NOT FOUND.\n- Drywall shop drawings: NOT FOUND as a requirement (only delegated design of openings >4 ft).\n- Architectural drawings of mockup details: NOT FOUND in the folder.\n"},"15_placa_yeso_materiales_instalacion.txt":{"titulo":"GYPSUM BOARD - MATERIALS AND INSTALLATION (092900; RFI-092900 = current version)","texto":"GYPSUM BOARD - MATERIALS AND INSTALLATION (092900; RFI-092900 = current version)\n\n1. PRODUCTS (2.2-2.3; V1 p758-760 / RFI-092900 p4-7)\n- \"Type X: ASTM C1396/1396M; 5/8-inch thick; fire resistant core\"\n- Moisture/mold resistant Type X 5/8\"; flexible 1/4\"; hi-impact; sound damping 5/8\"; sheathing 5/8\" Type X; cement board (summary; brands omitted).\n- Mullion trim caps (only A2/RFI-092900): STC >= 60; rated versions at rated walls (summary).\n- A8.1 N3: \"ALL GYP. BD. TO BE 5/8\" THICK TYPE 'X' UNO; PAINT ALL EXPOSED FACES.\"\n- A8.1 N4: glass-mat water-resistant backer Type X at wet walls; N5: MR Type X at \"HUMID\" locations incl. bathrooms.\n\n2. INSTALLATION (3.3; V1 p762 / RFI-092900 p8)\n- \"Install gypsum board to framing and furring members in accordance with manufacturer's recommendations, GA-216 or ASTM C840, and as specified in this Section.\"\n- RFI-092900 3.3.A.1: \"Install type X gypsum board at corridors.\" (V1/A2: \"hi-impact\")\n- Staggered end joints (stagger end joints).\n- \"Install 1/4 inch (nominal) above rough floor or curb.\"\n- Full height in one piece (summary).\n- \"... undercutting board 3/8 inch (nominal) and sealing top edge to adjacent surface with a continuous bead of sealant to form an elastic closure.\"\n- Corner beads at outside corners (summary).\n\n3. SCREWS: see 01_patron_tornillos.txt\n4. FIRE: see 07_fire_rated_2h_UL_GA.txt\n5. FINISHING/TOLERANCE: see 08_acabado_niveles_tolerancias.txt\n6. CONTROL JOINTS: see 09_juntas_de_control.txt\n\n7. SHEATHING AND CEMENT BOARD\n- 3.6.A: \"Install gypsum sheathing in accordance with GA-253 and manufacturer's instructions.\"\n- 3.7: cement board per ANSI A108.11 (summary).\n\n8. CONDITIONS (1.5-1.6)\n- Minimum temperature 40 F; 50 F for 48 h for finishing (summary).\n\n9. NOT FOUND\n- Board orientation (horizontal/vertical) per wall type: NOT FOUND.\n- Joint offset between layers (distance): NOT FOUND.\n"},"16_detalles_plafon_A3.21.txt":{"titulo":"GYPSUM CEILING DETAILS - SHEET A3.21 CEILING DETAILS (IFC; includes Addendum 2 and 3)","texto":"GYPSUM CEILING DETAILS - SHEET A3.21 CEILING DETAILS (IFC; includes Addendum 2 and 3)\n(file that arrived later; 1 page; text read from the PDF and checked against the image)\n\n1. GENERAL NOTES (A3.21)\n- N10: \"ALL WALLS EXTEND TO UNDERSIDE OF DECK EXCEPT THOSE SHOWN SHADED IN WHICH GYPSUM BOARD OR MASONRY EXTENDS MIN 4 INCHES ABOVE FINISHED CEILING. ALL METAL STUDS EXTEND TO UNDERSIDE OF FLOOR OR ROOF DECK.\"\n- N7: \"PROVIDE SUSPENSION SYSTEM AROUND ELECTRICAL FIXTURES, MECHANICAL GRILLES, DIFFUSERS, AND OTHER CEILING MOUNTED DEVICES.\"\n- N2: \"SEE SHEET SERIES A2 FOR UNIT REFLECTED CEILING PLANS.\"\n\n2. CEILING SYMBOLS (A3.21 Symbols)\n- \"GWB-1: GYPSUM CEILING ON METAL STUDS; SUSPENDED FROM RATED FLOOR/CEILING ASSEMBLY\"\n- \"GWB-2: GYPSUM CEILING TIGHT TO STRUCTURE; SEE HORIZONTAL ASSEMBLIES (SHEET A6.0)\"\n- \"GWB-3: 2HR RATED GYPSUM CEILING ON METAL STUDS\"\n- \"SPRAY APPLIED INSULATION AT UNDERSIDE OF STRUCTURE\"\n- \"CJ  CONTROL JOINT\"\n\n3. DETAILS\n- 1A \"LOW GYP. TO HIGH GYP.\" (1 1/2\" = 1'-0\"): \"3 5/8\" METAL STUD BRACING @ 32\" O.C.\" ; \"3 5/8\" METAL STUDS @ 16\" O.C.\" ; \"5/8\" GWB, TYP\" ; \"SUSPENDED GYPSUM BOARD CEILING\" ; \"CONTROL JOINT\"\n- 1B (low gypsum to high ceiling of another system): \"REFER TO A6 SERIES FOR JOIST ASSEMBLY\" ; \"STEEL FRAMING\" ; \"5/8\" GWB, TYP\" ; \"3 5/8\" METAL STUDS @ 16\" O.C.\" ; \"3 5/8\" METAL STUD BRACING @ 32\" O.C.\" ; \"CONTROL JOINT\"\n- 1C \"SUSP GWB CEILING EXP JT\": \"FACE OF CMU WALL\" ; \"1\" EJ\" ; \"CONT EXP JT COVER\"\n- 1D \"TYP SUSP GWB PERIMETER JT\": \"PERIMETER CASING BEAD - HOLD OFF THE WALL TO PROVIDE 1/4\" GAP\" ; \"BACKER ROD AND SEALANT\"\n- 1E \"TYPICAL GWB CEILING\": \"SEE PLAN FOR WALL TYPE\" ; \"METAL STUDS @ 16\" O.C., DEPTH TO RESULT IN DEFLECTION OF L/240 MAX, UNO\" ; \"GWB CEILING, SEE RCP FOR HEIGHT\"\n- 2A/2B \"SUSPEND GYPSUM CEILING CONTROL JOINT\" (A = \"PERPENDICULAR TO FURRING CHANNELS\", B = \"PARALLEL TO FURRING CHANNELS\"): \"HANGER WIRE, TYP\" ; \"LOOSE WIRE TIE, TYP\" ; \"1 1/2\" SUSPENDED CHANNELS, TYP\" ; \"SHORT PIECE OF FURRING CHANNEL INVERTED FOR SPLICE\" ; \"CONTINUOUS FURRING CHANNEL\" ; \"4\" MAX.\" (each side) ; \"1/4\"\" (gap) ; \"1 1/2\" CHANNEL PEICE, 16\" LONG, NESTED (TWO WIRE TIES EACH SIDE)\" ; \"GYPSUM BOARD CONTROL JOINT\"\n- 2C / 2D \"CEILING ACCESS PANEL @ GYP. BD.\" (3\" = 1'-0\"): \"PANEL WIDTH + 1/4\"\" ; \"MTL STUD, TYP\" ; \"1/2\" GWB\" ; \"DRYWALL COMPOUND AROUND OPENING PERIMETER\" ; 2D: \"SOUND ATTENUATION INSULATION - STRAP TO TOP OF ACCESS PANEL\"\n- 2E \"TYP. CEILING WALL CONNECTION\" (1 1/2\" = 1'-0\"): \"BATT INSULATION\" ; \"5/8\" TYPE 'X' GYPSUM WALL BOARD\" ; \"METAL STUD BOX HEADER\" ; \"2 1/4\" DIA SS SELF TAPPING SCREW @ 12\" O.C. - VERIFY W/ MANUF.\" (fastening at H.M. door frame) ; \"INSULATE DOOR FRAME CAVITY\" ; \"H.M. DOOR FRAME PER SCHEDULES\"\n\n4. NOT FOUND IN A3.21\n- UL design number of ceiling \"GWB-3: 2HR RATED\": NOT FOUND.\n- Screw pattern for board to furring/stud in ceiling: NOT FOUND.\n- Hanger / channel spacing in the detail: NOT FOUND (spec 092216 3.05: 48\" / 48\" / 16\" o.c.).\n- Referenced sheet A6.0 / A6 series and RCP (A2 series): not in the folder.\n"},"01_092900_Niveles_de_Acabado_GA-214.txt":{"titulo":"SECTION 09 29 00 – GYPSUM BOARD: JOINT TREATMENT AND FINISH LEVELS","texto":"SECTION 09 29 00 – GYPSUM BOARD: JOINT TREATMENT AND FINISH LEVELS\n========================\nCurrent version: 092900 GYPSUM BOARD – RFI-092900 (13 pp.), article 3.8 \"Joint Treatment and Finish Texture\", PDF pp. 10-11.\nHistory: subsection 3.8.G (levels with their location and number of coats) was added in RFI-092900. Table 3.8.H and subsections 3.8.A-F\nwere already in Manual V1 (pp. 764-765) and in Addendum 2 (p. 155).\nSource PDF: at the office (not included in the app) (earlier versions also copied, marked \"superseded\").\n\n3.8 A-F – GENERAL REQUIREMENTS\n  A. Finish per ASTM C840, GA-214 and GA-216.\n  B. Clean dust, oil and anything that prevents compound adhesion.\n  C. Set screws below the plane of the board.\n  D. Tape, fill and sand all joints, edges and corners to a smooth surface; fill dents and voids to achieve a monolithic surface.\n  E. Feather the compound coats onto the adjoining surface: maximum camber of 1/32\".\n  F. \"Spray apply texture on all gypsum board surfaces, unless noted otherwise.\" (sprayed texture everywhere unless noted; see file 03).\n\n3.8 G – LEVELS PER ASTM C840\n  Level 1 – Location: areas of fire-rated walls above finished ceilings, accessible or not.\n            Joints and interior angles embedded in compound; 1 coat of compound over joints, angles, screw heads and accessories.\n            Surface free of excess compound.\n  Level 2 – Location: (1) fire-rated, acoustic and smoke assemblies in plenums and concealed areas; (2) concealed areas and\n            construction not indicated as Level 3, 4 or 5. Embedded joints + 1 coat of compound. No excess compound.\n  Level 3 – Location: (1) walls with FRP panels; (2) areas with heavy texture before final paint; (3) areas with heavy commercial\n            wallcovering as final decoration; (4) storage rooms, utility rooms, riser closets, electrical and equipment rooms.\n            Embedded joints + 2 coats of compound; smooth compound free of tool marks; texture as indicated.\n  Level 4 – Location: walls and ceilings receiving paint or wallcovering. Embedded joints + 3 coats of compound; smooth, no marks.\n  Level 5 – Location: (1) walls with gloss, semi-gloss or enamel paint; (2) walls and ceilings with severe lighting and other indicated areas.\n            Embedded joints + 3 coats of compound; smooth; skim coat of \"high build gypsum board surfacer\" (high-build primer-surfacer).\n\n3.8 H – FINISH LEVELS PER GA-214 (table)\n  Area                                                                          | Finish\n  Plenums above ceilings                                                        | Level 1, no texture\n  Electrical and mechanical rooms                                               | Level 2, no texture\n  Moisture-resistant board as base (substrate for adhesive-applied finishes)    | Level 2\n  Light texture (medium orange-peel)                                            | Level 4\n  Smooth finish; satin/eggshell paint; use: rooms and offices                   | Level 4; Level 5 where there is critical (severe) lighting (see GA-214)\n  Smooth finish; semi-gloss paint; use: bathrooms and corridors                 | Level 5\n  Heavy-grade wallcovering                                                      | Level 3, no texture\n  Wallcovering                                                                  | Level 4, no texture\n\nPRIMER-SURFACER (2.3.F, p. 6): high-build interior finish applied by airless, used INSTEAD OF the Level 5 skim coat;\n  walls receiving it do not require drywall primer before the finish coats (brands omitted).\nRelation to painting (099123): the board must have ≤ 12 % moisture and the compound sanded smooth before painting (see file 06).\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed folio of the section).\n"},"02_092900_Cinta_Compuesto_Primer-Surfacer_y_Accesorios.txt":{"titulo":"SECTION 09 29 00 – TAPE, COMPOUND, PRIMER-SURFACER, ACCESSORIES AND BOARD PRODUCTS","texto":"SECTION 09 29 00 – TAPE, COMPOUND, PRIMER-SURFACER, ACCESSORIES AND BOARD PRODUCTS\n========================\nSource: 092900 GYPSUM BOARD – RFI-092900, Parts 1 and 2 (PDF pp. 3-7). Source PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\nBrands intentionally omitted.\n\nBOARD PRODUCTS (2.2 Gypsum Board, pp. 4-5; 2.4 Sheathing and 2.5 Cementitious Backer Board, p. 7)\n  - Type X board 5/8\" (ASTM C1396).\n  - Moisture-resistant Type X board 5/8\": absorption ≤ 5 % (ASTM C473); mold score ≥ 8 (ASTM D3273).\n  - Flexible board 1/4\" (curved walls).\n  - High-impact system (hi-impact): abrasion 1000 cycles; hard-body impact 115 ft-lb; soft-body 300 ft-lb (ASTM E695);\n    5/8\" board + 2 coats of veneer plaster.\n  - Sound-dampening board 5/8\": Type S/S12 screws, system sealant and putty.\n  - Sheathing (exterior): 5/8\" Type X with glass-fiber mat (ASTM C1177) or fiber-reinforced (ASTM C1278).\n  - Cement board 1/2\" [5/8\"]: No. 6 screws with 500 h salt spray (B117); mesh tape 2\" interior / 4\" exterior;\n    mortar ANSI A118.1/A118.4; 4 mil polyethylene barrier or 15# felt.\nACCESSORIES (2.3 Accessories, pp. 6-7; includes the following up to OTHER)\n  - Corner beads (corner bead) and edge trims (edge trim): ASTM C1047, galvanized steel.\n  - Control joint: zinc, with removable protected opening.\n  - Mullion trim caps (mullion trim caps) with STC ≥ 60 (added in Addendum 2).\nSCREWS (2.3)\n  - ASTM C1002, self-tapping, for framing less than 0.033\".\n  - ASTM C954 for framing from 0.033\" to 0.112\".\n  - For wood: type W, minimum penetration 3/4\".\nTAPE AND COMPOUND (2.3)\n  - 2\" paper tape, reinforced (heavy-duty), ASTM C475.\n  - Joint compound: ASTM C475 (per manufacturer; for base, fill and finish).\nPRIMER-SURFACER (2.3)\n  - High-build (high-build), applied by airless; used instead of the Level 5 skim coat. Walls receiving it do not need drywall primer.\nOTHER (2.3)\n  - Acoustic sealant per 079200. Intumescent pads (putty pads) of 7.25\" and 9\" for electrical boxes.\nSUBMITTALS AND QUALITY (1.3-1.5)\n  - Two 2' x 2' samples of the sprayed texture. Installer with 5 similar projects in the last 3 years.\nENVIRONMENTAL CONDITIONS (1.6, p.4)\n  - Minimum 40 °F during board installation.\n  - Minimum 50 °F for adhesives, joint treatment, texture and decoration, from 48 h before and continuously until dry. Provide ventilation.\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed folio of the section).\n"},"03_092900_Textura.txt":{"titulo":"SECTION 09 29 00 – TEXTURES","texto":"SECTION 09 29 00 – TEXTURES\n========================\nSource: 092900 GYPSUM BOARD – RFI-092900 (PDF pp. 3 [samples], 4 [conditions], 10-11 [3.8]). Source PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\n\nWhat the specification says about texture (everything that exists in the files):\n  - 3.8: \"Spray apply texture on all gypsum board surfaces, unless noted otherwise.\"\n    -> Sprayed texture on ALL gypsum board surfaces, unless noted otherwise.\n  - Level 3: areas with heavy texture before final paint; \"Surface to be coated texture as indicated\" (texture as indicated).\n  - Table H: light texture (medium orange-peel) = Level 4 with texture. No texture: plenums (N1), electrical/mechanical rooms (N2),\n    smooth paint in rooms/offices (N4/N5), semi-gloss paint in bathrooms/corridors (N5), wallcovering (N3/N4).\n  - Samples: two 2' x 2' samples of the sprayed texture (Part 1, submittals).\n  - Conditions: ≥ 50 °F for texturing from 48 h before until dry, with ventilation.\nNOT specified: texture product/manufacturer, exact pattern beyond \"medium orange-peel\" and texture location plan (see FALTANTES.txt).\nNote: there is a possible contradiction. 3.8 requires texture \"on all surfaces\" and table H requires smooth surfaces without texture in bathrooms,\ncorridors, rooms and offices. Transcribed as is; clarify with the architect.\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed folio of the section).\n"},"04_092900_Instalacion_Juntas_de_Control_y_Tolerancias.txt":{"titulo":"SECTION 09 29 00 – BOARD INSTALLATION, CONTROL JOINTS AND TOLERANCES (what affects finishing)","texto":"SECTION 09 29 00 – BOARD INSTALLATION, CONTROL JOINTS AND TOLERANCES (what affects finishing)\n========================\nSource: 092900 GYPSUM BOARD – RFI-092900, art. 3.1-3.7 and 3.9 (PDF pp. 7-12). Source PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\n\n3.1 EXAMINATION (p.7): verify framing, blocking, insulation and firestopping before installing board.\n3.2 FIRESTOPPING AND SEALANTS (p.8): putty pads at electrical boxes in rated walls.\n3.3 INSTALLATION (p.8)\n  - Type X board in corridors (RFI-092900 changed the Addendum 2 high-impact board in corridors to Type X).\n  - Moisture-resistant board in humid/wet areas; treat cut edges of MR board.\n  - Staggered end joints; board 1/4\" above floor; full height in one piece where possible.\n  - Where board runs full height to structure: cut it 3/8\" (nominal) short for deflection and seal the top edge with a continuous bead (elastic seal).\n  - Fasten with screws.\n  - Corner beads at all outside corners; edge trims (edge trim) where board meets dissimilar materials.\n  - CONTROL JOINTS at:\n      · building construction joints;\n      · walls over 30 linear feet;\n      · ceilings with perimeter relief: no more than 50 ft apart and areas of no more than 2500 ft²;\n      · ceilings without perimeter relief: no more than 30 ft apart and areas of no more than 900 ft²;\n      · exterior ceilings and soffits: no more than 30 ft and 900 ft²;\n      · changes in framing direction;\n      · transition from floor-supported framing to suspended framing.\n3.4 RATED ASSEMBLIES (p.9): per UL/GA-600 design; seal joints; firestopping per 078400.\n3.5 SOUND-DAMPENING BOARD (p.9): GA-201/216/600; perimeter gap of 1/4\" to 1/2\" with trim and acoustic sealant (ASTM C919);\n    screws at 16\" o.c. minimum; sealant bead of 1/8\" to 3/8\" at panel perimeter and at boxes; putty around boxes.\n3.6 SHEATHING (p.9): GA-253; screws 3/8\" from edges; do not countersink.\n3.7 CEMENT BOARD (p.10): ANSI A108.11; screws at 8\" o.c. maximum, 3/8\" from ends and 5/8\" from edges; heads flush; additional blocking where required.\n3.9 TOLERANCES (p.12)\n  - Flatness: maximum variation of 1/4\" in 10 ft in any direction.\n  - Substrate for ceramic tile: \"Refer to Section 093013\" (no numerical value was found in 093013; see FALTANTES.txt).\n  - Compound camber: maximum 1/32\" (3.8.E).\nEnvironmental conditions (1.6, p.4): ≥ 40 °F to install board; ≥ 50 °F for joint treatment, texture and decoration, from 48 h before until dry.\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed folio of the section).\n"},"05_092900_Historial_de_Versiones.txt":{"titulo":"SECTION 09 29 00 – VERSION HISTORY (to use the current one)","texto":"SECTION 09 29 00 – VERSION HISTORY (to use the current one)\n========================\nSource PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\n        [Source PDF at the office]\n        [Source PDF at the office]  <- CURRENT\n\n1. Manual V1, PDF pp. 755-765: original version. Already includes the GA-214 levels table (3.8.H), the sprayed texture \"on all surfaces\n   unless noted\" and the primer-surfacer instead of the Level 5 skim coat.\n2. Addendum 2, PDF pp. 145-155: adds mullion trim caps (mullion trim caps) and \"Install hi-impact gypsum board at corridors\"\n   (high-impact board in corridors).\n3. RFI-092900 (13-page document issued as the response to that RFI): changes to \"Install type X gypsum board at corridors\" (Type X board in corridors)\n   and adds subsection 3.8.G with the locations and number of coats of each level (1 to 5).\nFor finishing, the RFI-092900 version must be used.\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed folio of the section).\n"},"06_099123_Preparacion_de_Drywall_para_Pintura.txt":{"titulo":"SECTION 09 91 23 – INTERIOR PAINT REQUIREMENTS ON FINISHED GYPSUM BOARD","texto":"SECTION 09 91 23 – INTERIOR PAINT REQUIREMENTS ON FINISHED GYPSUM BOARD\n========================\nSource: Addendum 2, PDF pp. 165-167 (099123, art. 3.01-3.02) and p.171 (schedule G). Source PDF: at the office (not included in the app) (the complete section is in the source PDF, at the office)\n\n- Examination (3.01): maximum gypsum board moisture 12 % (electronic meter). Verify that the compound is sanded smooth;\n  do not start until unacceptable conditions are corrected.\n- Preparation (3.02): per MPI. Remove dust and chalk; patch (spackle) cracks and minor defects flush and sand.\n  If defects appear after the primer, patch again before the next coat.\n- Systems on board (3.06.G): walls with latex primer + 2 coats of acrylic latex enamel; ceilings with primer + 2 coats (flat);\n  wet areas pre-catalyzed acrylic epoxy; parking with exterior acrylic latex; under wallcovering, a primer that allows its removal.\n- If the 092900 primer-surfacer is used, walls receiving it do not need drywall primer (092900 2.3.F).\n- Semi-gloss/enamel sheens require Level 5 per 092900 (3.8.G and H). By default 099123 calls for eggshell on walls and semi-gloss on trim.\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed folio of the section).\n"},"07_092116.23_Shaft_Wall_Acabado.txt":{"titulo":"SECTION 09 21 16.23 – GYPSUM BOARD SHAFT WALL: PARTS THAT AFFECT FINISHING","texto":"SECTION 09 21 16.23 – GYPSUM BOARD SHAFT WALL: PARTS THAT AFFECT FINISHING\n========================\nSource: Manual V1, PDF pp. 739-744 (only version found). Source PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\nThe framing of this section is in the \"Framing specifications\" folder.\n\n- Conditions: per ASTM C840; install only in enclosed, conditioned spaces; reject wet or moldy panels.\n- Ratings per ASTM E119; acoustic tests E90/E413.\n- Faces: on the room side, gypsum board per 092900. On the shaft side, 1\" shaftliner: moisture-resistant Type X\n  (mold ASTM D3273 score 10, double bevel) or extended-exposure Type X (ASTM C1658, up to 12 months).\n- Trims (trim) per 092900. Screws ASTM C1002.\n- Control joints per ASTM C840. Isolate the perimeter and seal with acoustic sealant (079219).\n- Sloped cap panels (cant panels) at projections over 4\": 1/2\" or 5/8\" board, angle ≥ 75°, screws at 24\" o.c., framing at 24\" o.c.\n- Tolerance: 1/8\" (see text in PDF).\n- Joint treatment and finish level on the room side are governed by 092900 3.8 (file 01).\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed folio of the section).\n"},"08_Notas_de_Acabado_en_Planos.txt":{"titulo":"GYPSUM BOARD FINISHING NOTES ON DRAWINGS","texto":"GYPSUM BOARD FINISHING NOTES ON DRAWINGS\n========================\nCopies (1 p. each) at the office: [PDF at the office], [PDF at the office],\n[PDF at the office]\nEnglish text as it appears, with translation.\n\nA8.1 Partition Types and Schedules (revision RFI-A8.1)\n  - Note 3: \"ALL GYP. BD. TO BE 5/8\" THICK TYPE 'X' UNO; PAINT ALL EXPOSED FACES.\" -> 5/8\" Type X board unless noted; paint all exposed faces.\n  - Unique conditions in the type key: 2 = SAB (acoustic insulation); 3 = resilient channel + SAB; 4 = tile backer (tile backer board);\n    8 = tile backer + air/vapor barrier. They indicate where cement board or tile backer board goes (finishing per 092900 3.7 and 093013).\nA3.21 Ceiling Details (IFC)\n  - Note 10: walls to the underside of the slab, except shaded walls, where the board extends 4\" above the ceiling\n    (Level 1 or 2 above ceilings per 092900).\n  - Ceiling types: GWB-1 (board on studs suspended from the rated floor/ceiling assembly), GWB-2 (tight to structure, see A6.0),\n    GWB-3 (2 h rated, on metal studs).\n  - Detail 1C: 1\" expansion joint with continuous cover.\n  - Detail 1D: perimeter casing bead held 1/4\" off the wall, with backer rod and sealant.\n  - Detail 2A: ceiling control joint with 1/4\" gap.\n  - Details 2C/2D: access panel with compound at the opening perimeter; opening = panel width + 1/4\".\n  - Note 11: paint at amenities with exposed structure (see painting folder).\nA7.10 Stair and Elevator Details (IFC)\n  - \"GWB ON METAL STUDS BETWEEN STRINGERS AND ALONG BEAM/BELOW LANDING - SEE SECTIONS\"\n  - \"CASING BEAD AND 1/4\" SEALANT WHERE GWB OCCURS\" -> casing bead and 1/4\" sealant where there is board.\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed folio of the section).\n"},"08_acabado_niveles_tolerancias.txt":{"titulo":"FINISHING (LEVELS 1-5) AND TOLERANCES - SECTION 092900","texto":"FINISHING (LEVELS 1-5) AND TOLERANCES - SECTION 092900\n\n1. GENERAL RULES (3.8; V1 p764 / RFI-092900 p10)\n- \"Finish gypsum board surfaces in accordance with ASTM C840, GA-214 and GA-216.\"\n- \"Set mechanical fasteners below the plane of the board.\"\n- \"Tape, fill, and sand all joints, edges and corners to produce smooth surface ready to receive finishes.\"\n- \"Feather coats onto adjoining surfaces so that camber is maximum 1/32-inch.\"\n- \"Spray apply texture on all gypsum board surfaces, unless noted otherwise.\"\n- Tape (2.3.D): \"ASTM C475/C475M; 2 inch wide heavy duty paper joint tape.\" ; Compound (2.3.E): \"ASTM C475/C475M.\"\n- Primer-surfacer (2.3.F): \"used in lieu of skim coat in a Level 5 finish ... applied with an airless sprayer.\"\n\n2. DEFINITION OF LEVELS (only in RFI-092900 3.8.G, p10-12)\n- Level 1: \"Fire-resistance-rated wall areas above finished ceilings, whether or not accessible in the completed construction.\" / \"Apply one coat joint compound over joints, angles, fastener heads, and accessories.\"\n- Level 2: \"Fire-rated, sound-rated, and smoke-rated assemblies in ceiling plenums and concealed areas.\" / \"Use at concealed areas and construction not indicated to be Levels 3, 4, or 5.\" / one coat.\n- Level 3: \"Walls to receive FRP panels.\" ; \"Areas to receive heavy texture finishes before final painting.\" ; heavy duty wallcoverings ; \"storage, service rooms, riser closets, electrical rooms, and equipment rooms.\" / \"Apply 2 coats joint compound\"\n- Level 4: \"Walls and ceilings to receive paint finish or wall coverings.\" / \"Apply 3 coats joint compound over joints, angles, fastener heads, and accessories.\" / \"Smooth and free of tool marks and ridges.\"\n- Level 5: \"Walls with glossy, semigloss, and enamel finishes.\" ; \"Walls and ceilings to receive areas of severe lighting and other areas specifically indicated.\" / 3 coats / \"Skim Coat: High build gypsum board surfacer.\"\n\n3. TABLE BY AREA (GA-214) (V1 p764-765 3.8.G / RFI-092900 p12 3.8.H)\n- \"Plenum areas above ceilings.\" -> \"Level 1 finish, no texture.\"\n- \"Electrical and mechanical rooms.\" -> \"Level 2 finish, no texture.\"\n- \"Moisture resistant gypsum backing board (substrate for adhesive applied finish material).\" -> \"Level 2 finish.\"\n- \"Light texture (medium orange-peel).\" -> \"Level 4 finish.\"\n- \"Smooth finish; satin/eggshell paint finish; use: classrooms and offices.\" -> \"Level 4 finish. Level 5 finish where critical (severe) lighting condition occurs (refer to GA-214 ...)\"\n- \"Smooth finish; semi-gloss paint finish; use: restrooms and corridors.\" -> \"Level 5 finish.\"\n- \"Heavy-grade wall covering.\" -> \"Level 3 finish, no texture.\"\n- \"Wall covering.\" -> \"Level 4 finish, no texture.\"\n\n4. TOLERANCES\n- 092900 3.9.A (V1 p765 / RFI-092900 p12): \"Maximum variation from true flatness: 1/4 inch in 10 feet in any direction.\"\n- 092900 3.9.B: substrate for ceramic tile: \"Refer to Section 093013.\"\n- 092216 3.04.H (V1 p753) and 092116.23 3.03.J (V1 p743): framing \"vary not more than 1/8 inch from the plane formed by faces of adjacent framing.\"\n- 092216 3.05.F (V1 p754): suspension \"level to within 1/8 inch in 12 feet\".\n- 092900 3.3.C: \"Install 1/4 inch (nominal) above rough floor or curb.\"\n\n5. SAMPLES\n- 092900 1.3.C (V1 p757 / RFI-092900 p3): \"Submit two 2 foot by 2 foot samples of spray-applied texture finish.\"\n\n6. NOTES / NOT FOUND\n- Conflict to review (not interpreted): RFI-092900 Level 2 includes \"Fire-rated ... assemblies in ceiling plenums\" while the table gives \"Plenum areas above ceilings\" = Level 1.\n- Finish drawings indicating \"severe lighting\" or specific Level 5 areas: NOT FOUND.\n"},"01_099123_Pintura_Interior.txt":{"titulo":"SECTION 09 91 23 – INTERIOR PAINTING (INTERIOR PAINTING)","texto":"SECTION 09 91 23 – INTERIOR PAINTING (INTERIOR PAINTING)\n========================\nCurrent version: Addendum 2, PDF pp. 162-172 (supersedes Manual V1, pp. 840-849).\nDifference detected V1 -> Addendum 2: only subsection 3.06.J \"Special requirements for corridors\" was added (the rest of the text is the same).\nSource PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\nNote: manufacturers and commercial product names from the source are intentionally omitted here; consult them in the source PDF.\n\n1. SCOPE (1.02, p.162)\n   Surface preparation and application of paint systems on interior substrates:\n   concrete, concrete block (CMU), steel and iron, galvanized metal, wood and gypsum board (gypsum board).\n   Related (p.163): shop priming of structural steel (Div. 05), 055000 Metal Fabrications,\n   055116 plate stairs, 055213 pipe railings, 099600 High-Performance Coatings.\n   Sustainability requirements: CDPH v1.2-2017 (emissions), VOC limits CARB 2007 SCM / SCAQMD Rule 1113.\n\n2. SUBMITTALS (1.03-1.04, p.163)\n   - Product data for each product, with VOC content.\n   - Samples for initial selection (each type of final finish) and verification samples\n     per system, color and sheen: on 8\" x 8\" rigid backing, applied in steps showing each coat, labeled.\n   - Product list cross-referenced to systems and locations, with color designations.\n   - Maintenance data (cleaning, touch-up, repair) and Coatings Maintenance Manual\n     (summary by area with finish schedule, where each product/color/finish was used, technical data sheets,\n     MSDS, cleaning, touch-up procedure and color samples).\n   - Extra material: paint 5 %, but not less than 1 gallon of each material and color applied.\n\n3. QUALITY AND MOCK-UPS (1.05, p.164)\n   - Applicator with a minimum of 5 years of experience.\n   - Mockups of each system and each color/finish: vertical and horizontal surfaces of at least 100 ft².\n     Final color approval is based on the mockups.\n\n4. STORAGE AND FIELD CONDITIONS (1.06-1.07, p.164)\n   - Store at not less than 45 °F in closed, ventilated containers.\n   - Apply only with surface and ambient temperature between 50 °F and 95 °F.\n   - Do not apply with relative humidity > 85 %, at less than 5 °F above the dew point, or on damp surfaces.\n\n5. PRODUCTS – GENERAL (2.01-2.04, p.164-165)\n   - Basis-of-design product per the schedule, or equivalent from one of 3 listed manufacturers.\n   - All products within the same system must be from the same manufacturer, \"no exceptions\".\n   - Primers: compatible with the finish; when the manufacturer offers options, use the one rated \"best\".\n   - Colors and sheen: per drawings; anything not indicated is selected by the architect.\n     By default: EGGSHELL FINISH ON WALLS and SEMI-GLOSS ON TRIM (moldings/frames).\n   - Do not paint: factory-finished items, rating labels, stainless steel/anodized aluminum/bronze,\n     natural stone, floors (unless indicated), tile, glass, acoustic materials (unless indicated),\n     concealed piping/ducts/conduit.\n\n6. EXAMINATION (3.01, p.165)\n   Maximum moisture content (electronic meter): concrete 12 %; masonry/CMU 12 %; wood 15 %; gypsum board 12 %.\n   Gypsum board: verify that the compound (finishing compound) is sanded smooth.\n\n7. PREPARATION (3.02, p.166-167)\n   - Follow the manufacturer and the \"MPI Architectural Painting Specification Manual\".\n   - Steel: remove rust, mill scale and shop primer; minimum cleaning SSPC-SP 2.\n   - Shop-primed steel: clean field welds, connections and abraded areas; touch up with the same\n     material as the shop primer per SSPC-PA 1.\n   - Galvanized: degrease mechanically to a clean, lightly etched surface.\n   - Wood: seal knots, sand, prime all faces, fill with putty and sand.\n   - Gypsum board: remove dust/chalk; patch (spackle) cracks and minor defects flush and sand; if after the\n     primer defects appear, patch again before the next coat.\n\n8. APPLICATION (3.03, p.167)\n   - Paint behind movable furniture the same as exposed surfaces; behind fixed equipment primer only.\n   - Paint both faces of access panels and hinged covers.\n   - Tint each undercoat a lighter shade to distinguish coats; apply additional coats if it shows through.\n   - Paint where exposed (equipment rooms): equipment and panels, uninsulated metal and plastic piping,\n     supports, metal and plastic conduit, tanks without final finish, insulation with paintable jacket.\n     In occupied spaces: the same (except tanks) plus whatever the architect indicates.\n   - Paint the interior of unlined ducts visible behind grilles.\n\n9. QUALITY CONTROL (3.04, p.168): the owner may engage dry film thickness (DFT) testing;\n   if it does not comply, the contractor pays for the test and applies additional coats.\n\n10. INTERIOR PAINT SCHEDULE (3.06, p.168-172) – all with \"Two Top Coats\" (2 finish coats) over primer\n   A. Concrete (non-traffic):\n      - General: latex primer for concrete + 2 coats of acrylic latex enamel.\n      - Public restrooms, fitness/lockers, tanning, janitor rooms, pet wash, laundry, trash and similar:\n        concrete primer + 2 coats of single-component pre-catalyzed acrylic epoxy (water-based).\n      - Parking interior: concrete primer + 2 coats of exterior acrylic latex.\n   B. CMU:\n      - General: latex block filler + 2 coats of acrylic latex enamel.\n      - Wet areas (same list as above): block filler + 2 coats of pre-catalyzed acrylic epoxy.\n      - Parking interior: latex block filler + 2 coats of exterior acrylic latex.\n   C. Exposed structural steel: high-performance polyurethane system from Section 099600.\n   D. Steel – metal fabrications, railings, hollow metal doors and frames (hollow metal):\n      touch-up with rust-inhibitive primer (if shop-primed) or anticorrosive primer (if not)\n      + 2 coats of pre-catalyzed acrylic epoxy.\n      Metal deck/roof deck (pre-primed): manufacturer’s shop primer + 2 coats of water-based acrylic \"dry fall\".\n   E. Galvanized – ducts: galvanized primer + 2 coats of water-based industrial acrylic enamel.\n      Galvanized roof deck: 2 coats of water-based acrylic dry fall.\n   F. Wood – trim and doors: wood primer + 2 coats of pre-catalyzed acrylic epoxy.\n   G. Gypsum board:\n      - Walls: latex primer + 2 coats of acrylic latex enamel.\n      - Ceilings: latex primer + 2 coats of acrylic latex (the listed products are flat/matte finish).\n      - Wet areas (public restrooms, fitness/lockers, tanning, janitor, pet wash, laundry, trash,\n        interior faces of exterior walls marked with epoxy and similar): primer + 2 coats of pre-catalyzed acrylic epoxy.\n      - Parking interior: primer + 2 coats of exterior acrylic latex.\n      - Under wallcovering (wall covering): acrylic latex primer that allows the wallcovering to be removed without damaging the board paper.\n   H. Special parking requirements: stall lines and accessible markings; number at each stall;\n      BRIGHT WHITE PAINT (white gloss) on all drywall walls and ceilings; CMU clean and unpainted;\n      stenciled level number at least 4 ft high at each stair and elevator lobby;\n      signage package (Level 1, Level 2..., SLOW DOWN, Building entry, Stairwell Entry, Future Resident parking).\n   I. Special unit (apartment) requirements: 1 accent wall in the living room and 1 per bedroom;\n      EGGSHELL on walls, SEMI-GLOSS on trim, FLAT (matte) on ceilings.\n   J. Special corridor requirements (added in Addendum 2): all systems minimum 2 coats\n      (more if indicated); walls eggshell; trim semi-gloss.\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed folio of the section).\n"},"02_099113_Pintura_Exterior.txt":{"titulo":"SECTION 09 91 13 – EXTERIOR PAINTING (EXTERIOR PAINTING)","texto":"SECTION 09 91 13 – EXTERIOR PAINTING (EXTERIOR PAINTING)\n========================\nSource: Manual V1, PDF pp. 833-839 (only version found; it does not appear in Addendum 2, Addendum 3 or the RFIs reviewed).\nSource PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\nManufacturers and trade names intentionally omitted (see source PDF).\n\n1. SUBMITTALS (1.03-1.04, p.834)\n   - Product data with VOC content; 8\" x 8\" selection and verification samples on rigid backing,\n     applied in steps showing each coat and labeled; product list with location and color.\n   - Coatings maintenance manual (same as 099123).\n   - Extra material: 5 % but not less than 1 gallon of each material and color.\n2. QUALITY (1.05): applicator with 5 years minimum; mockups of each system/color of at least 100 ft².\n3. COORDINATION (1.06, p.835): coordinate finishes with shop primers; intermediate coats if required for compatibility.\n4. STORAGE: minimum 45 °F.\n5. FIELD CONDITIONS (1.08): apply between 50 °F and 95 °F (surface and ambient); do not apply in snow, rain,\n   fog or mist, RH > 85 %, within 5 °F of the dew point, or on a damp surface.\n6. PRODUCTS (2.01-2.03, p.835-836)\n   - Basis of design per schedule or equivalent from one of 3 listed manufacturers.\n   - Scope: paint all exterior surfaces exposed to view, except factory-finished or indicated as unfinished.\n     Do not paint: factory finish, labels, non-metallic roofing and flashing, stainless/anodized/bronze, stone, floors,\n     tile, glass, acoustic materials, concealed piping/ducts.\n   - A single manufacturer per system (no exceptions) and, where possible, throughout the project.\n   - Manufacturer’s \"best\" primer. Colors and sheen per drawings or architect’s selection.\n7. EXAMINATION (3.01, p.836): maximum moisture concrete 12 %, masonry/CMU 12 %, wood 15 %; cement plaster fully cured.\n8. PREPARATION (3.02, p.836-837): MPI; steel SSPC-SP 2; shop primer touch-up per SSPC-PA 1; galvanized\n   mechanical degreasing; fiber cement clean and within allowed moisture/alkalinity; wood: exterior knot sealer, sand, prime all faces, patch.\n9. APPLICATION (3.03, p.837-838)\n   - Paint BOTH FACES AND EDGES OF EXTERIOR DOORS and all exposed surfaces of exterior door frames.\n   - Paint all exposed surfaces of window frames and sashes.\n   - Undercoats tinted a lighter shade; additional coats if it shows through.\n   - Paint where in view: equipment and panels, uninsulated metal and plastic piping, supports, conduit, tanks without final finish.\n10. QUALITY CONTROL (3.04, p.838): dry film thickness tests at the owner’s discretion.\n11. EXTERIOR PAINT SCHEDULE (3.06, p.838-839)\n   A. Concrete – miscellaneous non-traffic surfaces: high-build acrylic system:\n      water-based alkali-resistant primer + 2 coats of high-build exterior acrylic, DFT not less than 10 mils.\n      Concrete – vertical surfaces (walls and columns): waterproof elastomeric coating from Section 099653.\n   B. Single-wythe CMU (single wythe): elastomeric coating from Section 099653.\n   C. Exposed structural steel: high-performance polyurethane system from Section 099600.\n   D. Steel and iron: light industrial acrylic system: touch-up with rust-inhibitive primer (if shop-primed)\n      or exterior anticorrosive primer (if not) + 2 coats of exterior light industrial acrylic.\n   E. Galvanized: exterior galvanized primer + 2 coats of exterior light industrial acrylic.\n   F. Wood (trim): exterior latex wood primer + 2 coats of exterior acrylic latex.\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed folio of the section).\n"},"03_099600_Recubrimientos_Alto_Desempeno.txt":{"titulo":"SECTION 09 96 00 – HIGH-PERFORMANCE COATINGS (HIGH-PERFORMANCE COATINGS)","texto":"SECTION 09 96 00 – HIGH-PERFORMANCE COATINGS (HIGH-PERFORMANCE COATINGS)\n========================\nSource: Manual V1, PDF pp. 850-857 (only version found).\nSource PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\nManufacturers and trade names intentionally omitted (see source PDF).\n\n1. SCOPE (1.2, p.850): EXTERIOR substrates: concrete, CMU, steel and galvanized metal.\n   Related: Div. 05 (shop priming of structural steel with the primers of this section), 055213 railings,\n   099113 and 099123 (general painting). 099113 and 099123 refer to this section for EXPOSED STRUCTURAL STEEL.\n2. SUBMITTALS (1.3-1.4): product data with VOC; 8\" x 8\" step samples; product list; maintenance data and manual;\n   extra material 5 % and not less than 1 gallon per material and color.\n3. QUALITY (1.5, p.852)\n   - Applicator with 5 years.\n   - MANUFACTURER’S TECHNICAL REPRESENTATIVE: attends preconstruction meetings, examines substrates, witnesses tests and inspects the final installation; submits written reports.\n   - Mockups of at least 100 ft² (walls and ceilings) + finish repair sample (preparation, feathering, texture and color).\n4. CONDITIONS (1.7-1.8): store at 45 °F min.; apply between 50 °F and 95 °F; RH ≤ 85 %; ≥ 5 °F above dew point; do not apply exterior in snow, rain, fog or mist.\n5. WARRANTY (1.9, p.853): manufacturer’s special warranty against corrosion for 5 YEARS from substantial completion;\n   failure = corrosion greater than 1 % of the area per ASTM D 610.\n6. PRODUCTS (2.1-2.4, p.853-854)\n   - Basis of design per schedule or equivalent from one of 4 listed manufacturers; all coats of a system from the same manufacturer.\n   - Compatible joint sealant: single-component non-sag urethane, ASTM C 920 Type S, Grade NS, Class 25, Use NT (±25 % movement).\n   - 100 % solids epoxy filler and patching compound for steel imperfections.\n7. EXAMINATION (3.1): maximum moisture concrete 12 %, masonry 12 %.\n8. PREPARATION (3.2, p.854-855)\n   - Fill with patching epoxy the steel imperfections VISIBLE FROM 10 FT away.\n   - Fill with sealant joints and interfaces with poor paint coverage.\n   - Steel: minimum cleaning SSPC-SP 10 / NACE No. 2. Shop primer: touch-up per SSPC-PA 1.\n   - Surfaces receiving zinc-rich primer: SSPC-SP 11 preparation before primer.\n   - Galvanized: mechanical degreasing, lightly etched surface.\n9. APPLICATION (3.3): per manufacturer and MPI; tinted undercoats; free of defects (holidays, runs, etc.).\n10. QUALITY CONTROL (3.4): technical representative attends tests and inspections; DFT tests at the owner’s discretion.\n11. TOUCH-UPS (3.5.D, p.856): zinc-primed steel: prepare with SSPC-SP 11 (power tool to bare metal); feather edges;\n    profile well-adhered existing paint and apply tie coat with the system primer.\n12. EXTERIOR SCHEDULE (3.6, p.856-857)\n   A. Concrete: elastomeric coatings per 099653.\n   B. CMU: elastomeric coatings per 099653.\n   C. STEEL: pigmented polyurethane over organic zinc-rich primer and high-build epoxy (zinc minimum 80 % in dry film):\n      - Primer: zinc-rich, organic, 2.5 to 3.5 mils DFT.\n      - Intermediate coat: high-build epoxy, low gloss, 5.0 to 8.0 mils DFT.\n      - Topcoat: two-component polyurethane, pigmented, gloss, 2.0 to 3.0 mils DFT.\n   D. GALVANIZED: pigmented polyurethane system:\n      - Primer: epoxy for metal, 2.0 to 4.0 mils DFT.\n      - Topcoat: two-component polyurethane, pigmented, gloss, 2.0 to 4.0 mils DFT.\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed folio of the section).\n"},"04_099653_Recubrimientos_Elastomericos.txt":{"titulo":"SECTION 09 96 53 – ELASTOMERIC COATINGS (ELASTOMERIC COATINGS)","texto":"SECTION 09 96 53 – ELASTOMERIC COATINGS (ELASTOMERIC COATINGS)\n========================\nSource: Manual V1, PDF pp. 858-867 (only version found).\nSource PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\nManufacturers and trade names intentionally omitted (see source PDF).\n099113 refers vertical concrete walls/columns and exterior single-wythe CMU here.\n\n1. SCOPE (1.2, p.858): exterior concrete and concrete block (CMU) substrates.\n2. SUBMITTALS (1.3-1.4, p.859): product data with VOC; 8\" x 8\" samples on the same type of substrate, in steps\n   (including primers and block filler); product list; maintenance manual; extra material 5 % and not less than 1 gallon\n   per material, color and texture (from the same batch).\n3. QUALITY (1.5, p.860): applicator 5 years; mockups of at least 100 ft² + finish repair sample.\n4. CONDITIONS (1.6-1.7): store at 45 °F min.; apply between 50 °F and 90 °F (unless the manufacturer allows otherwise);\n   do not apply in snow, rain, fog, RH > 85 %, within 5 °F of the dew point or on damp surfaces.\n5. WARRANTY (1.8, p.861): 10 YEARS from substantial completion against water penetration through the coating and deterioration beyond normal weathering.\n6. MATERIALS (2.3, p.861-862): minimum vapor transmission 10 perms (ASTM D1653); products from the same manufacturer per system;\n   crack fillers, alkali-resistant primer and high-performance latex block filler recommended by the manufacturer.\n7. EXAMINATION (3.1, p.862): substrate moisture ≤ 12 %; start no sooner than 28 days (or what the manufacturer says, whichever is stricter)\n   after the substrate is built and visually dry on both sides; alkalinity within the manufacturer’s range.\n8. PREPARATION (3.2): MPI; cleaning; repair cracks before coating.\n9. APPLICATION (3.3, p.863): each coat separately; primer and block filler at full coverage rate (pores filled);\n   number of coats and total DFT per manufacturer and as needed for the warranty; undercoats tinted;\n   SPRAYING only if allowed by the authority, and do NOT \"double back\" to form two coats in one pass.\n10. QUALITY CONTROL (3.4): the owner may sample materials and verify installed thicknesses.\n11. SCHEDULE (3.6, p.864-867)\n   Y. CONCRETE:\n      1. Silicone elastomeric system: waterproof, breathable, smooth texture, minimum total DFT 10 mils.\n         Primer per manufacturer + 100 % water-based silicone intermediate coat (min. 5 mils DFT) + silicone topcoat (min. 5 mils DFT).\n      2. Acrylic elastomeric system: smooth texture; primer per manufacturer + elastomeric acrylic intermediate 4.0-8.0 mils DFT\n         + elastomeric acrylic topcoat 4.0-8.0 mils DFT (DFT and number of coats as needed for the warranty).\n      2 (sic). High-build acrylic system (hi-build): primer per manufacturer + topcoat 12.0-15.0 mils DFT,\n         with 10 or fewer pinholes (pinholes) per ft².\n   Z. CMU: the same three systems, with BLOCK FILLER instead of primer in the silicone and elastomeric acrylic systems\n      (the hi-build on CMU indicates \"Prime Coat\" per manufacturer).\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed folio of the section).\n"},"05_Imprimado_de_taller_Div05_y_Puertas_081113.txt":{"titulo":"SHOP PRIMING (SHOP PRIMING) AND TOUCH-UPS – DIV. 05 STEEL AND DOORS/FRAMES 08 11 13","texto":"SHOP PRIMING (SHOP PRIMING) AND TOUCH-UPS – DIV. 05 STEEL AND DOORS/FRAMES 08 11 13\n========================\nThese sections are not painting sections, but they set the primer that arrives from the shop and refer to 099113/099123/099600\nfor finishing and touch-ups. The current versions are used (Addendum 3 where it exists; otherwise Manual V1).\nSource PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\n  (contains Addendum 3 pp. 152,153,155,156,160,161,162,165,168,187,191,192,195,282,284 and Manual V1 pp. 311,315,321,323,329, in that order)\n\n05 12 00 STRUCTURAL STEEL (Addendum 3, pp. 148-156)\n  - Coordinate shop primers with finishes (p.148). Primer: comply with 099113, 099123 and 099600 (2.4, p.152).\n  - Galvanizing repair paint: MPI#18, MPI#19 or SSPC-Paint 20 per ASTM A 780 (p.152).\n  - 2.8 Shop priming (p.153): prime everything except surfaces embedded in concrete (extend primer 2\" on partially embedded pieces),\n    surfaces to be field welded, slip-critical bolted connections and surfaces receiving sprayed fireproofing.\n    Preparation SSPC-SP 2 or SSPC-SP 3. Primer with minimum DFT 1.5 mils; one-coat non-asphaltic primer per SSPC-PS Guide 7.00, ≥1.5 mils.\n  - Touch-up after erection with the same shop material per SSPC-PA 1 (p.155); cleaning and primer touch-up: per 099600 (p.156).\n\n05 21 00 STEEL JOISTS (Addendum 3, pp. 157-162)\n  - Primer SSPC-Paint 15 or equivalent manufacturer’s standard (p.160). Galvanizing repair MPI#18/19 or SSPC-Paint 20 (p.161).\n  - Cleaning SSPC-SP 2 or SP 3; one coat of shop primer, continuous film not less than 1 mil (p.161).\n  - Touch-ups: per 099113 and 099123 (p.162).\n\n05 31 00 METAL DECK / STEEL DECK (Addendum 3, pp. 163-168)\n  - Composite deck: manufacturer’s standard baked-on rust-inhibitive gray or white primer (p.165), or G60 galvanized.\n  - Galvanizing repair per ASTM A 780; primer repair with primer of the same color; wire brushing and touch-up of the underside per 099113/099123 (p.166-168).\n  - (099123 specifies for interior roof deck 2 coats of acrylic \"dry fall\".)\n\n05 50 00 METAL FABRICATIONS (Addendum 3, pp. 182-199)\n  - Paint compatibility certificates from finish manufacturers over the shop primers (p.184).\n  - Primers (p.187): comply with 099113/099123/099600; water-based anticorrosive primer MPI#107 (distinguishable from the zinc one);\n    zinc-rich epoxy primer MPI#20; galvanized primer for exterior use; repair paint SSPC-Paint 20; bituminous paint ASTM D 1187.\n  - Shop prime non-galvanized iron/steel (except embedded in concrete, with fireproofing or masonry) with water-based primer\n    or universal primer from 099113 (p.191).\n  - Zinc coating mandatory at exteriors: pieces to be field painted = zinc-rich primer; unpainted pieces = galvanized (p.192).\n  - Preparation: exteriors, pieces with zinc primer or with 099600 primers: SSPC-SP 6/NACE No. 3 (commercial blast); others: SSPC-SP 3.\n    Apply per SSPC-PA 1 with \"stripe coat\" at corners, crevices, bolts, welds and edges (p.192).\n  - Touch-up after erection with the same shop material, SSPC-PA 1 (p.195).\n\n05 51 16 PLATE STAIRS (Manual V1, pp. 306-317; not found in addenda)\n  - Primers per 099113 and 099123; modified alkyd universal primer MPI#79; zinc primer SSPC-Paint 20 Type I-A Level 1 (p.311).\n  - Preparation SSPC-SP 6/NACE No. 3 (also interior stairs); shop primer per SSPC-PA 1 with stripe coat (p.315).\n  - Sheet A7.11: paint railings, stringers (stringers), guards and exposed underside of ramps and landings (see file 07).\n\n05 52 13 PIPE RAILINGS (Manual V1, pp. 318-325)\n  - Primers per 099113/099123/099600; water-based MPI#107; zinc epoxy MPI#20 (p.321).\n  - Railings: shop zinc primer to receive field finish; preparation SSPC-SP 6 at exteriors, with zinc primer or with 099600 primers; SSPC-SP 3 elsewhere (p.323).\n\n05 70 00 DECORATIVE METAL (Manual V1, pp. 326-332)\n  - Repair paint SSPC-Paint 20; zinc epoxy primer MPI#20; high-performance coating: polyurethane system from 099600 (p.329).\n\n08 11 13 HOLLOW METAL DOORS AND FRAMES (HOLLOW METAL) (Addendum 3, pp. 272-284)\n  - Field painting per 099113/099123 (p.272). Exposed faces: rust-inhibitive shop primer, lead- and chromate-free,\n    complying with ANSI/SDI A250.10 (p.282).\n  - Touch-up immediately after erection: sand rusted/damaged areas and apply compatible inhibitive primer, zinc primer\n    (exteriors and galvanized) or finish paint (p.284).\n  - Field finish per 099123: primer + 2 coats of pre-catalyzed acrylic epoxy (see file 01).\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed folio of the section).\n"},"06_017700_Material_Extra_y_Manual_de_Mantenimiento_Pintura.txt":{"titulo":"SECTION 01 77 00 – CLOSEOUT (CLOSEOUT): EXTRA MATERIAL AND PAINT MAINTENANCE MANUAL","texto":"SECTION 01 77 00 – CLOSEOUT (CLOSEOUT): EXTRA MATERIAL AND PAINT MAINTENANCE MANUAL\n========================\nSource: Addendum 3, PDF pp. 14-15 (Section 017700, article 1.5 B, items 11 and 12).\nSource PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\n\n- Item 11 – 099113 Exterior painting: paint 5 %, but not less than 1 gallon of each material and color applied;\n  and the Coatings Maintenance Manual.\n- Item 12 – 099123 Interior painting: same requirement (5 %, minimum 1 gallon per material and color) and the Maintenance Manual.\n- Contents of the Coatings Maintenance Manual (as in 099113/099123 1.04): summary by area with the finish schedule;\n  detail by area (product, color and finish used); product data; safety data sheets (MSDS/SDS); care and cleaning instructions;\n  touch-up procedure; color samples of each color and finish.\n- The section mentions \"attic stock\" per an \"Appendix S\"; that appendix is NOT in the available files (see FALTANTES.txt).\n- Sections 099600 and 099653 require extra material on their own: 5 % and not less than 1 gallon per material and color (099653: also per texture).\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed folio of the section).\n"},"07_Pintura_en_Divisiones_MEP_ManualV2.txt":{"titulo":"PAINTING REQUIREMENTS WITHIN THE MEP SECTIONS (Manual V2)","texto":"PAINTING REQUIREMENTS WITHIN THE MEP SECTIONS (Manual V2)\n========================\nSource: Manual V2 (Divisions 21-33). Source PDF: at the office (not included in the app)\n(7 pages: V2 p.8, 60, 61, 82, 102, 126 and 236, in that order). Trade names omitted.\n\n21 00 00 – Fire protection (V2 p.8, art. 1.8 PAINTING)\n  - Exposed piping (dry columns/standpipes and drains in stairs, parking decks, etc.): clean, primer\n    and 2 coats of enamel; color to be selected by the architect. Touch up damaged factory finishes.\n22 00 00 – Plumbing, general requirements (V2 p.60-61, art. 5.7 PAINTING)\n  - Finish painting is specified in another section; the plumber delivers clean surfaces and touches up damaged factory finishes.\n  - 1 coat of anticorrosive primer on all non-galvanized ferrous steel (supports, hangers) and unfinished ferrous piping.\n  - Match the original finish in touch-ups; do not paint identification plates (nameplates).\n22 16 00 – Gas piping (V2 p.82, art. 3.2)\n  - Exposed ferrous gas piping: primer + 2 coats of exterior enamel; color subject to approval.\n23 00 00 – HVAC, general requirements (V2 p.102, art. 3.13)\n  - Painting is done by Division 9; the HVAC contractor does the touch-ups of factory finishes.\n  - Ducts visible through grilles: interior painted FLAT BLACK.\n  - 1 coat of anticorrosive paint (brand product with 5-year warranty, name omitted) on supports, hangers and unfinished ferrous piping.\n  - Do not paint identification plates.\n23 07 00 – HVAC insulation (V2 p.126)\n  - \"Where indicated, painting of insulation jackets shall be per Section 099100 - Painting\" (that section does NOT exist in the manual; see FALTANTES.txt).\n  - (V2 p.129, not copied) jackets with a paintable finish.\n26 00 00 – Electrical, general requirements (V2 p.236, art. 3.05 Z/AA)\n  - Painting per the painting section; touch up damaged factory finishes.\n32 31 19 – (V2 p.472, not copied): touch-up per SSPC-PA 1.\nRelationship with 099123 (art. 3.03): in equipment rooms and occupied spaces, exposed equipment, piping, supports, conduit and insulation are painted.\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed folio of the section).\n"},"08_Notas_de_Pintura_en_Planos_A8.1_A3.21_A7.11.txt":{"titulo":"PAINTING NOTES ON DRAWINGS","texto":"PAINTING NOTES ON DRAWINGS\n========================\nSources (1-page copies of each at the office):\n  - Sheet A8.1 Partition Types and Schedules (revision RFI-A8.1) -> [PDF at the office]\n  - Sheet A3.21 Ceiling Details (IFC)                          -> [PDF at the office]\n  - Sheet A7.11 Stair/Elevator details (IFC)                   -> [PDF at the office]\nText quoted verbatim (English) with explanation.\n\nA8.1, general note 3:\n  \"ALL GYP. BD. TO BE 5/8\" THICK TYPE 'X' UNO; PAINT ALL EXPOSED FACES.\"\n  -> All gypsum board shall be 5/8\" Type X unless noted otherwise; PAINT ALL EXPOSED FACES.\n\nA3.21, note 11:\n  \"IN AMENITY AREAS WITH EXPOSED STRUCTURE PAINT ALL EXPOSED SLAB, MECH EQUIP., ELEC. EQUIP., AND PLUMBING. REFER TO RCP FOR\n   PAINT COLOR. PAINT ALL EXPOSED MECHANICAL EQUIPMENT WITH A TRANSPARENT EPOXY PRODUCT. REFER TO SF-02 IN ID FINISH SCHEDULE FOR SPEC.\"\n  -> In amenity areas with exposed structure: paint exposed slab, mechanical and electrical equipment and plumbing; color per\n     reflected ceiling plan (RCP); exposed mechanical equipment with a transparent epoxy product; specification in SF-02 of the interior\n     design finish schedule (that schedule is NOT available).\nA3.21 also shows the paint tag \"PT-101\" (its legend/color is not in the files).\n\nA7.11:\n  \"PAINT STL HANDRAILS, STRINGERS, GUARDRAILS, UNDERSIDE OF EXPOSED STAIR RUN AND LANDINGS\"\n  -> Paint steel handrails, stringers, guardrails and the exposed underside of stair runs and landings\n     (system per 099123 D: primer + 2 coats of pre-catalyzed acrylic epoxy).\n  Page numbers are PDF pages (not the printed folio of the section).\n"},"paredes_2h_fire_rated.txt":{"titulo":"2-HOUR FIRE RATED WALLS - DRYWALL","texto":"2-HOUR FIRE RATED WALLS - DRYWALL\nSource: partition types sheet (read only). No project, company or person names.\n\n1. 2-HOUR STUD WALLS\n- Types: S2-3-40, S2-6-40 and S2-6-41\n- Two layers of 5/8\" Type X gypsum board on each side\n- Approximate thickness: S2-3-40 = 6 1/8\"; S2-6-40 and S2-6-41 = 8 1/2\"\n\n2. 2-HOUR SHAFTS\n- Types: H2-4-30 and H2-6-30\n- Two layers on one side and one layer on the other\n- Over 1\" shaft liner\n- Approximate thickness: H2-4-30 = 6 1/4\"; H2-6-30 = 7 1/4\"\n\n3. MATERIAL\n- All gypsum board is 5/8\" Type X\n- Studs at 16\" o.c.\n- At shafts, studs at 24\" o.c.\n\n4. SHEET NOTE\n- Rated walls form a continuous separation from floor to floor,\n  with no breaks at columns, beams, transitions or other obstructions.\n- At rated conditions, penetrations are sealed with fire caulking.\n\n5. SCREWS\n- The spec refers to the GA-216 standard and the indicated UL design.\n- The screw pattern (edge and field, per layer) NOT YET FOUND.\n\n6. LOCATION ON DRAWINGS\n- Where these walls go NOT YET FOUND.\n\nPENDING\n- Screw pattern, UL/GA numbers, location on drawings and RC1.\n"}},"faltantes":{"framing":"MISSING – FRAMING SPECIFICATIONS\n========================\nI searched the project documents (Manual V1, Manual V2, Addendum 2, Addendum 3, 092900 RFI-092900 and sheets A3.21, A7.2, A7.3, A7.4, A7.10, A7.11, A7.12 and A8.1).\nThe following does NOT exist or is NOT in the available files:\n\n1. Section 06 10 00 \"Rough Carpentry\": does NOT exist. Rough carpentry is in 061053 (included). There is 061600 Sheathing,\n   which was not included because it is wood sheathing and not framing/blocking.\n2. Limiting height tables (limiting heights) or gauge by height/span tables: neither 092216 nor A8.1 gives a table. A8.1 only gives the L/240 at 5 psf criterion,\n   25 gauge at acoustic walls and \"no limiting height if braced to adjacent construction\".\n3. Gauge/thickness by wall type in the A8.1 schedule: the columns for tested assemblies (UL/GA numbers) and for top/bottom of wall details\n   are empty or show \"UL ###\".\n4. Head clip movement in 092216: it says \"allow vertical movement\" without a dimension. In 054000 the deflection track leg\n   was left as unedited text (\"Insert dimension…\").\n5. 054000 has no product articles for load-bearing walls, floor joists or rafters (the Summary does include them) and gives no spacing\n   (it says \"As indicated on Drawings\").\n6. There are no structural drawings (S series), architectural plans (A1/A2, where load-bearing studs are marked with hatching), wall sections,\n   A6 horizontal assemblies (cited in A3.21 for GWB-2), nor shop drawings or delegated-design calculations for the framing.\n7. Sections 05 12 00 and 05 21 00 (structural steel and joists) were not included because they are not stud framing. They are current in Addendum 3.\n8. UL design numbers for rated walls and fire-resistant joints ([state code] 2015 §706): not in the files.\n9. Wood framing (type W in the A8.1 key): there is no structural wood framing section nor W types in the schedules.\n10. Details 2B, 3 and 5 of A8.1 and those of A7.10/A7.12 can be seen in the copied PDF. Their graphic content (drawing dimensions) was not fully transcribed,\n    only the legible text.\n\nNote: the source PDFs stay at the office (their headers carry the project name, company and city). Only the clean text goes in the app.\n","insulacion":"PENDING (from the file 12_aislamiento_acustico.txt)\n7. NOT FOUND\n- 072100 (V1 p426) and 092116.23 (V1 p742) refer to 092900 for \"sound attenuation blanket\", but 092900 does not specify it: NOT FOUND.\n","drywall":"MAIN MISSING ITEMS (from the file 00_indice_fuentes.txt; detail in each file)\n- Edge/field screw pattern per layer: NOT FOUND (refers to UL / GA-600 / GA-216 / C840)\n- UL/GA design numbers of the rated walls: NOT FOUND (blank columns in A8.1; \"UL ###\" at concrete)\n- Term \"RC1\" / \"RC-1\" for resilient channel: NOT FOUND (RC-1 in V2 p113 is \"Roof Curb, Type 1\")\n- Floor plans with the location of each wall type: NOT FOUND (there are no plans in the folder)\n- Head-of-wall / top-of-wall details (\"T.O. WALL DETAIL\" columns in A8.1 blank): NOT FOUND\n","finishing":"MISSING – FINISHING SPECIFICATIONS (DRYWALL FINISHING)\n========================\nI searched the project documents (Manual V1, Manual V2, Addendum 2, Addendum 3, 092900 RFI-092900, 096519 RFI-096519 and sheets A3.21, A7.2, A7.3, A7.4,\nA7.10, A7.11, A7.12 and A8.1). The following does NOT exist or is NOT in the available files:\n\n1. Product, manufacturer and texture pattern: it only says \"spray texture on all surfaces unless noted\", \"medium orange-peel\"\n   (Level 4) and \"heavy texture\" (Level 3). No product is specified and there is no texture location plan.\n   There is a possible contradiction: 3.8.F calls for texture everywhere and table 3.8.H calls for a smooth finish in restrooms, corridors, lounges and offices.\n2. Drawing or schedule assigning finish level by room: there is no finish plan, RCP with levels nor interior design schedule (ID Finish Schedule).\n   Levels are only defined by use type (092900 3.8.G/H).\n3. Definition of \"critical/severe lighting\" on the project: 092900 refers to GA-214, but does not say which walls have it.\n4. Text of the GA-214, GA-216 and ASTM C840 standards: they are only cited; there is no copy in the files.\n5. Numerical substrate tolerance for tile: 092900 3.9.B refers to 093013 and no numerical value was found there.\n6. Veneer plaster section (veneer plaster, 09 26 00) or plastering section (09 24 00): does NOT exist. Veneer plaster only appears as part of the high-impact system in 092900.\n7. Screw spacing for standard wall and ceiling board: not given. It is only given for sound-deadening board (16\" o.c.), cement board (8\" o.c.),\n   sheathing (3/8\" from the edge) and shaft wall cant panels (24\" o.c.). It refers to C840/GA-216.\n8. Tape and compound brands and models: only the ASTM C475 standard and \"2\" heavy-duty paper tape\" are specified.\n   There is no compound type (setting vs. premixed) per coat.\n9. Section 09 22 16 as finishing: not applicable (it is framing; it is in the framing folder).\n10. Additional finishing RFIs or submittals: apart from RFI-092900, no drywall finishing RFIs, submittals or mockups were found.\n\nNote: the source PDFs stay at the office (their headers carry the project name, company and city). Only the clean text goes in the app.\n","pintura":"MISSING – PAINTING SPECIFICATIONS\n========================\nI searched the project documents (Manual V1, Manual V2, Addendum 2, Addendum 3, RFI-092900 of 092900, RFI-096519 of 096519 and sheets\nA3.21, A7.2, A7.3, A7.4, A7.10, A7.11, A7.12 and A8.1). The following does NOT exist or is NOT in the available files:\n\n1. Sections 09 90 00, 09 91 00 \"Painting\" and 09 93 00 \"Staining and Transparent Finishing\": they are not in the Manual V1 index nor in the addenda.\n   Painting is divided into 099113 (exterior), 099123 (interior), 099600 and 099653.\n   230700 (Manual V2 p.126) refers to a \"Section 099100 - Painting\" that does NOT exist.\n2. The color and finish schedule: the interior design finish schedule (ID Finish Schedule, including SF-02), the legend and color of \"PT-101\",\n   the RCP colors and the finish plans/interior elevations (A2/A6/ID series) are not available.\n   Accent walls and colors are not defined in the files.\n3. The attic stock \"Appendix S\" cited in 017700 (Addendum 3): not available.\n4. MPI system numbers (e.g. \"MPI INT 9.2A\"): the 099113 and 099123 schedules give product and number of coats, not MPI number.\n5. Dry film thicknesses (DFT) per coat for 099113 and 099123: not given (except \"not less than 10 mils\" on miscellaneous exterior concrete).\n   Only 099600 and 099653 give DFT.\n6. Specific sheen/finish per room: there are only defaults (eggshell on walls, semi-gloss on trim), units (flat on ceilings),\n   corridors and parking (white gloss). There is no sheen schedule per room.\n7. The anti-graffiti coating mentioned in section 014339 (Addendum 2) is not specified in 099600 nor in any other section.\n8. The \"tile-like coatings\" (tile-like coatings) that 099123 says are specified in 099600: 099600 does not include them.\n9. Floor/traffic paint: there is no floor painting section. The traffic coating (071800) and resinous flooring (096723)\n   were not included because they are not paint.\n10. Wood varnishes/stains (stain/clear finish): no system is specified; 099123 only has epoxy enamel over wood.\n11. The DFT test report, approved submittals and color samples: there are no field documents (painting submittals/RFIs) in the files.\n12. Mockups and warranties: they only exist as a written requirement. No evidence was found that they were done.\n\nNote: the source PDFs stay at the office (their headers carry the project name, company and city). Only the clean text goes in the app.\n","fire2h":"PENDING (from the file paredes_2h_fire_rated.txt)\n- Screw pattern, UL/GA numbers, location on drawings and RC1.\n"}};
